{"offset": 0, "next": 100, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b1bb4ada8f1114ccf7a9e2774b3d0f0beedc7609", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09318", "CorpusId": 295660338}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b1bb4ada8f1114ccf7a9e2774b3d0f0beedc7609", "title": "Recursively Self-Improving Electromagnetic Spectrum Agents: Architecture and Operation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09318, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2219241939", "name": "Bao-Lin Chong"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Dongfang Xu"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Qiao-Si Wang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Fu-Hui Zhou"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Qi-Hui Wu"}], "abstract": "Electromagnetic spectrum agents (ESAs) must contend with changing environments and task requirements during sustained autonomous operation, yet their existing capabilities may not remain suitable for new operating conditions. This article proposes recursively self-improving electromagnetic spectrum agents (RSI-ESAs) that use operational feedback to update their capabilities and build on prior improvements. We present their architecture and editable components, and describe a self-improvement process encompassing problem diagnosis, candidate generation, validation, and inheritance. By retaining effective task versions and using modification records and diagnostic knowledge to guide subsequent updates, RSI-ESAs can accumulate task capabilities and improvement experience across successive rounds. A shared-spectrum scheduling case study illustrates improved task performance and capability accumulation over multiple improvement rounds under the tested conditions. Finally, we discuss open challenges for broader tasks and long-term operation, outlining research directions for the continued capability development of ESAs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "49fbac3361d3c956614e7188c08f3334b8375c25", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07319", "CorpusId": 295593945}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/49fbac3361d3c956614e7188c08f3334b8375c25", "title": "WeightLoop: Weight-Space Programming with Experimental Feedback", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07319, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2395829104", "name": "Meng-Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2342563132", "name": "Bo Li"}, {"authorId": "2465905593", "name": "Wei-Jing Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2470149799", "name": "Cheng-Yang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2527027461", "name": "Bo-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2504271058", "name": "Jin-Feng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2401262031", "name": "Ze-Lin Zang"}, {"authorId": "2504599073", "name": "Victor C. M. Leung"}, {"authorId": "2254827517", "name": "S. Li"}], "abstract": "Weight-space programming (WSP) shows that language models can reason about and rewrite neural network weights; recursive self-improvement (RSI) motivates feedback-driven improvement. Models guiding experiments receive concrete feedback: completed assays reveal prediction errors and inform what to test next. We introduce WeightLoop, a framework that turns experimental feedback into persistent updates to deployed predictors. GPT-6 Astra inspects a task specification and the model implementation, then writes a bounded, reusable parameter-editing program. As new measurements arrive, the program identifies prediction discrepancies, validates candidate edits using observed outcomes, and writes the selected changes back to the original model. The editor remains fixed while the predictor evolves, enabling repeated updates without further LLM calls or gradient retraining while preserving the original inference interface. In offline replay, targeted edits improve mean ranking utility in seven of eight endpoint-budget settings (all eight versus random edits) and mean final discovery across three sequential tasks. WeightLoop connects WSP with an RSI-inspired improvement loop, making experimental evidence a direct driver of model change and subsequent decisions."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07624", "CorpusId": 295594843}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "title": "Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07624, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2526809778", "name": "Min Tang"}, {"authorId": "2505784112", "name": "Jun-Bo Bai"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2525002153", "name": "Qing-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3aceea8bec22ee7bd89fabc75b66c9fe6981edb2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06768", "CorpusId": 295588863}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3aceea8bec22ee7bd89fabc75b66c9fe6981edb2", "title": "PyRUA-REAL: Robot Exploration and Adaptive Learning through RSI in the Real World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06768, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506666634", "name": "Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2190428643", "name": "Jian-Xin Bi"}, {"authorId": "2385327322", "name": "Shu-Long Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2383166153", "name": "Rui-Yang Si"}, {"authorId": "2311570567", "name": "Wen-Bo Huang"}, {"authorId": "2286953962", "name": "Kai-Qi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2445495190", "name": "Jia-Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2107939582", "name": "Li-Yun Li"}, {"authorId": "2504354514", "name": "Xuan-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2404260527", "name": "Da-Quan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Coding agents powered by models such as GPT-6-Astra connect spatial reasoning with executable robot behavior, raising the question of how such agents can produce lasting improvements in robot capabilities from physical interaction. In this work, we study embodied recursive self-improvement (RSI) through real-world exploration, code refinement, and deployment using PyRUA-REAL, a framework in which coding agents retain interaction experience as persistent memory and reusable executable skills that inform subsequent exploration and improvement. Our empirical study places physical manipulation at the center of the improvement process, examining how agents collaborate, what kinds of execution feedback support effective code refinement, and how task specifications and previously acquired skills influence robustness and task transfer. We further investigate the efficiency and capability of the resulting programs when deployed without involving an online agent for decision-making. Across eight real-world tasks, PyRUA-REAL achieves an average success rate of 91.1%. In complementary evaluation on 42 tasks from the RoboDojo benchmark, it achieves an average success rate of 84.4%. This study provides empirical guidance for turning physical exploration into reusable robot skills that support continued improvement and robust, efficient deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9488d31c9be1376ca43673709ae84471fbf87cd6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04277", "CorpusId": 295506456}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9488d31c9be1376ca43673709ae84471fbf87cd6", "title": "Harness-S: Learning from Sibling Harnesses for Generalizable Agent Training", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04277, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258697236", "name": "Kang-Ning Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2387790741", "name": "Wen-Xiang Jiao"}, {"authorId": "2526099633", "name": "Bo-Wen Fang"}, {"authorId": "2353075566", "name": "Shuai Shao"}, {"authorId": "2143405615", "name": "Jiachen Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2304552745", "name": "Rongying Shan"}, {"authorId": "2303893413", "name": "Shi-Jian Wang"}, {"authorId": "2353314544", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2432661987", "name": "Yuan Lu"}, {"authorId": "2375764292", "name": "Wei-Wen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2401625176", "name": "Wei-Nan Zhang"}], "abstract": "Harness evolution from a common base exhibits Locality, Complementarity, and Nonstationarity: variants repair different failures, and their utility changes as the policy learns. Rather than seeking a universally superior harness, we introduce Harness-S to selectively rescue failures of a base execution system through sibling harnesses and transfer the resulting capabilities into a single policy that remains effective under the original harness. Directional Harness Evolution develops specialized sibling harnesses along distinct repair directions, forming a reusable Sibling Harness Pool that remains fixed during policy training. Complementary Harness Rescue Training invokes this pool only when all initial attempts under the base harness fail. The same policy generates fresh rollouts under selected auxiliary harnesses, and these trajectories replace the failed group for reinforcement learning. An Adaptive Harness Router selects the Top-k variants using Harness Behavior Profiles and voting across multiple LLMs. Policy-aware profile re-anchoring refreshes this evidence as the policy learns. Across AppWorld, SWE-Gym, and DBBench, the trained policy substantially outperforms policy-only training when returned to the original base harness. Behavioral analyses and held-out cases support the transfer of harness-elicited behaviors into the policy itself. Reusing the router for Rank-1 inference provides additional gains at the same executor rollout count."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04437", "CorpusId": 295506501}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "title": "WiSI: Wireless-in-the-Loop Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04437, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Engineering", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2238140933", "name": "Zong-Xi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2363514324", "name": "Hong-Yang Du"}], "abstract": "The growing autonomy of AI agents has enabled self-improvement, where agents iteratively refine their programs through execution feedback and performance evaluation. As these agents increasingly operate in physical environments and rely on wireless connectivity, communication conditions become an integral part of their improvement process. However, existing approaches largely assume ideal communication, overlooking how wireless conditions shape both the feedback that guides program modifications and the evaluation outcomes that determine which modifications survive. This challenge is further complicated by the coupling between agent behavior and wireless connectivity, as agents influence their own communication conditions through mobility and transmission decisions. To address this problem, we propose WiSI, a wireless-in-the-loop self-improvement framework that integrates spatiotemporal wireless channels, edge-assisted perception, and Large Language Model-driven program improvement. By incorporating wireless execution into the improvement loop, WiSI reveals how wireless execution experience shapes the generation and retention of program structures, and investigates the wireless conditions needed for robust improvement. Experiments show that agents improved under ideal links achieve only 40.0% task success under congestion, compared with 77.2% when improved under realistic wireless conditions. More importantly, wireless-aware feedback and selection jointly enable agents to discover and retain communication-efficient program structures that outperform parameter tuning and remain robust even with inaccurate run-time channel observations. We further find that moderate-load or mixed-network evaluation maintains strong performance across deployment conditions, while simplified models preserving link-rate statistics achieve comparable results without detailed spatial channel information."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8fc7172dc514e7004fd14bca5029e231d0a18ae8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05320", "CorpusId": 295506857}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8fc7172dc514e7004fd14bca5029e231d0a18ae8", "title": "PyRUA-DataFarm: LLM Agent as a Cost-Efficient Teleoperator for Real-Ready Robot Data", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05320, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362922379", "name": "Ke-Wei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367736494", "name": "Jun Ma"}, {"authorId": "2526102471", "name": "Guo-Dong Lv"}, {"authorId": "2341662997", "name": "Duo-Min Wang"}, {"authorId": "2480056713", "name": "Fei-Xiang Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2383166153", "name": "Rui-Yang Si"}, {"authorId": "2385947083", "name": "Chuan-Rui Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2526363415", "name": "Yuxuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2526346210", "name": "Hao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2526372657", "name": "Yi-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2440685250", "name": "Yintianrun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2269764462", "name": "Yu-Fan Deng"}, {"authorId": "41020000", "name": "En-Ze Xie"}, {"authorId": "2404260527", "name": "Da-Quan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Teleoperation remains the default way to collect robot demonstrations, but every episode costs robot and operator time, and every new task starts from scratch. We ask whether a coding agent can take over the teleoperator's job. In our DataFarm, the agent receives a data request and one calibration episode of the robot in the target scene and rebuilds the task in simulation: it writes the scene, an expert controller, and a success judge. The simulator supplies exact actions, and a video model conditioned on real images renders each rollout as photographic video, generating all camera views together consistently. The output is real-ready data: synchronized camera views labelled with the robot's actions, on which a policy can be trained directly and deployed on the real robot. Independent judges certify the expert and every rendered episode before admission. On five real-robot tasks, policies trained only on farm data match policies trained on as many teleoperated episodes (93 vs. 84 successes in 100 trials), and under an unseen tablecloth and coloured light they keep 87 successes where the teleoperation-trained policies fall to 46. Adapting a task is fully automatic and costs \\$21 of API usage on average, paid once per task, plus \\$5 for a render contract; every further episode costs \\$0.80 on average for simulation, rendering, and review, with no robot or operator. One hour of three-view data for a task costs \\$107 on average, these costs included, slightly below a quote of \\$117 for teleoperation in China and below \\$290 in labour alone in North America."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03178", "CorpusId": 295503623}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "title": "CoworkOS:Scaling Agent Coworkers for Real-world Automation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03178, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "4376097", "name": "Jiajie Jin"}, {"authorId": "2316562231", "name": "Guan-Ting Dong"}, {"authorId": "2499629513", "name": "Cheng-Jun Pan"}, {"authorId": "2526383219", "name": "Xin Deng"}, {"authorId": "2455322947", "name": "Zhuo-Hui Sheng"}, {"authorId": "2486909710", "name": "Guo-Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2352016379", "name": "Tao Gui"}, {"authorId": "1900406", "name": "Yutao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2526363415", "name": "Yuxuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "30000816", "name": "Shi-Hong Yi"}, {"authorId": "2365380853", "name": "Bin-Ze Hu"}, {"authorId": "2382833241", "name": "Zhi-Xiong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2495119597", "name": "Zhi-Chao Sun"}, {"authorId": "2375822021", "name": "Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2503895275", "name": "Han Han"}, {"authorId": "2526383123", "name": "Ming-Yu Ma"}, {"authorId": "2499651393", "name": "Jun-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "2526390285", "name": "Xiao-Tao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2495439365", "name": "Hui Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2302980850", "name": "Xin-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2282543731", "name": "Wanghan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2391873065", "name": "Ya-Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2513111", "name": "Zhecan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269420111", "name": "Honglin Guo"}, {"authorId": "2343324260", "name": "Xiao-Yu Xing"}, {"authorId": "2522801396", "name": "Xiao-Qiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2455424509", "name": "Yu-Lun Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2144456832", "name": "Xiaoxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2399060433", "name": "Jia-Hang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2362570366", "name": "Shao-Hua Liu"}, {"authorId": "2042683163", "name": "Shi-Han Dou"}, {"authorId": "2218237934", "name": "Zhi-Heng Xi"}, {"authorId": "2374299112", "name": "Qi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2394203475", "name": "Yu-Gang Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2241348826", "name": "See-Kiong Ng"}, {"authorId": "2468147446", "name": "Ya-Pan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2257036129", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}, {"authorId": "2186578511", "name": "Ji-Rong Wen"}, {"authorId": "2257039188", "name": "Zhi-Cheng Dou"}], "abstract": "Rapid advances in cowork agents are shifting human-AI collaboration toward real-world automation, delivering software, designs, and other artifacts. However, such productivity work demands both high-quality delivery on complex productivity tasks and continued monitoring and decision-making over long horizons, a combination that remains an open challenge. We introduce CoworkOS, a general-purpose harness for long-horizon work that separates task strategy from execution management: the model decides actions, delegation, and acceptance, while the runtime maintains state and feedback. Its three-layer architecture enables direct execution or delegation to Subagents, synchronously or asynchronously, under stable responsibilities. Through a shared application layer, humans and agents inspect and revise the same evolving documents, presentations, CAD models, and Blender scenes, so human-in-the-loop participation can enter both through conversation and through direct modification of the work itself. To sustain continuity across agents and sessions, CoworkOS further introduces hierarchical workspaces and persistent semantic memory, so working files, key decisions, and long-running Job logs remain shareable, reusable, and resumable after interruption. Across 12 benchmarks and 6 categories of real-world applications, CoworkOS proves highly competitive with frontier agent applications such as Codex and DeepSeek Harness. As a sustained-work case study, CoworkOS uses GLM-5.3 as its backbone to conduct RSI-driven automated post-training on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B. Over five rounds, the model's average score across eight benchmarks rose from 33.19 to 42.95 (+9.76). Looking ahead, we believe CoworkOS opens a meaningful path toward general cowork-agent intelligence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6c6fe4a0fbf445aa4e4e12b909a300eec2c136f6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02797", "CorpusId": 295502389}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6c6fe4a0fbf445aa4e4e12b909a300eec2c136f6", "title": "SelfImprove-Bench: Evaluating Generalization in Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02797, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2382941198", "name": "Bang-Rui Xu"}, {"authorId": "2526194103", "name": "Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2479245941", "name": "Si-Di Chen"}, {"authorId": "2403662638", "name": "Xuan-He Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2289911484", "name": "Jian-Tao Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2493634270", "name": "Qian-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2499852383", "name": "Zi-Jie Wang"}, {"authorId": "2257429642", "name": "Fan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2325139162", "name": "Zhoufutu Wen"}], "abstract": "Recursively self-improving (RSI) systems are increasingly viewed as a promising means of accelerating progress toward artificial general intelligence. They aim to autonomously improve their capabilities by refining their working pipelines and recursively improving the strategies that guide subsequent refinements, with gains generalizing to unseen scenarios.However, existing evaluations lack reliable metrics to distinguish capability gains from scenario fitting, sustainable and extensible protocols for unseen tasks and difficulties, and fine-grained attribution of improvement to evolution traces. To this end, we propose SelfImprove-Bench,an RSI evaluation framework that organizes tasks into three primary capabilities (i.e., Exploring, Planning, Reasoning) as scenarios for guiding model evolution. We instantiated the tasks on games for controllability and reproducibility, and pairs them with scalable generation and orchestration driven by task templates and difficulty coefficients. Beyond task-level scores, SelfImprove-Bench organizes evaluation into generalization ladder levels G0 to G4 that measure how far gains transfer from training conditions. Experiments reveal that the evolutionary gains of existing RSI methods are inconsistent across generalization levels."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04035", "CorpusId": 295507243}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "title": "Recursa: Toward Recursively Self-Improving Foundation Model Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04035, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2253476816", "name": "Er-Kang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2117033724", "name": "Yin Lin"}, {"authorId": "2330462706", "name": "Xiao-Rong Shi"}, {"authorId": "2300408961", "name": "Xuan-Rui Lin"}, {"authorId": "2237499232", "name": "Jing-Ren Zhou"}], "abstract": "Foundation models in changing environments can encounter unfamiliar domain knowledge that their original training did not cover. However, new domains rarely provide large amounts of expert-created training data or reliable supervisory signals for further training. We introduce Recursa, a foundation model system that adapts to new domains by learning from its own experience without human experts or a stronger teacher model. Recursa uses a knowledge repository to store what it learns: domain-specific skills and instructions on when to apply them, along with memories of past task experiences and reflections on what to improve. Recursa bootstraps the repository from domain materials and then refines it through recursive self-improvement. It uses parallel learners to execute tasks and synthesize local improvements, then validates and merges the improvements into the knowledge repository. On Harvey LAB's Firm Knowledge benchmark, Recursa raises the all-pass rate on held-out test tasks from 30.0% to 55.0% with DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and from 35.0% to 46.0% with Qwen3.8-Flash."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "63d959da4a821d417df1715f692ab66bff8263d6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01113", "CorpusId": 295502232}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/63d959da4a821d417df1715f692ab66bff8263d6", "title": "CogRSI: Evolving and Auditing Cognitive Reasoning Strategies", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01113, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2321032244", "name": "Yan-Dan Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2408472160", "name": "Hao-Ran Luo"}, {"authorId": "1637883582", "name": "Xin-Liang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "1755919", "name": "A. L\u01b0u"}], "abstract": "Can a language model learn better ways to reason about other people's minds by improving its own strategies, and are the strategies it learns actually right? We study this with cogrsi. A grounded reader marks who saw each event in a story and uses a short written principle to settle disagreements between its readings. With a principle written by experts, it beats self-consistency at the same token cost. A self-improvement loop then writes the principle itself. It keeps an update only if accuracy rises by a calibrated margin, and in one variant only if it also passes counterfactual test pairs. The learned principles transfer: on 800 BigToM pairs that no system had seen, each of them scores higher than the expert principle. We then check what the learned rules actually say. Each rule is translated into code and run on thousands of small stories with known answers. Most of them give a wrong answer in at least one situation they claim to cover, for example by treating a character who is in the room as knowing what happened even while asleep. Accuracy does not reveal which rules are wrong, and the counterfactual test does not fix this. A strategy can help in practice and still be wrong as a rule, so both need checking. Our code and data are publicly available."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d51b97caca5bb44d1b9dd475488a1ac8e72bb896", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.00960", "CorpusId": 295503740}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d51b97caca5bb44d1b9dd475488a1ac8e72bb896", "title": "Self-Refining Prompt Induction for Streaming Text-to-SQL", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00960, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "3415700", "name": "Gaetano Rossiello"}, {"authorId": "1711133", "name": "A. Gliozzo"}, {"authorId": "2689774", "name": "Nandana Mihindukulasooriya"}, {"authorId": "2526372846", "name": "Erick Lee"}, {"authorId": "2526029236", "name": "Nabila Siregar"}, {"authorId": "2282996559", "name": "Lisa Amini"}], "abstract": "Text-to-SQL assistants for streaming engines must handle dialect-specific types, windowing rules, and temporal semantics, including constructs poorly handled by generic prompts. We present Self-Refining Prompt Induction (SRPI), an offline method that converts generation failures into reusable dialect instructions while keeping model weights fixed. A Refiner revises the Generator's prompt using reference-conditioned feedback from three LLM judges; a sampled regression guard checks earlier successes, and a separate Compressor reduces prompt length. Inference uses the frozen prompt without retrieval or query repair. We evaluate cross-domain and cross-model transfer on Confluent Cloud Flink SQL using a controlled synthetic workload seeded by 30 expert-written examples, with 335 induction examples and 257 questions from held-out domains. The same induced prompt improves mean judge scores across seven Generators by 7.1-19.2 percentage points, including an increase from 85.3% to 97.5% for the induction model. A separate compression study reduces prompt length by 52.7% with a 0.19-percentage-point score decrease. An assistant using the induced prompt is in early access, with initial user feedback to inform subsequent refinement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41216", "CorpusId": 295402364}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "title": "TinyAstral: Transferring Self-Evolving Harnesses to Tiny Generalist Manipulation Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2397760867", "name": "Ke-Hong Gong"}, {"authorId": "1753669202", "name": "Xiao-Xu Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2437476734", "name": "Guan-Ling Hou"}, {"authorId": "2525389666", "name": "Seung-Hun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2507875926", "name": "Meng-Qi Guo"}, {"authorId": "151506836", "name": "Run-Jia Tan"}, {"authorId": "2398983002", "name": "Zheng-Yu Wen"}, {"authorId": "2398589143", "name": "Qi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2274713895", "name": "Xiao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2397823150", "name": "Ming-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "153122790", "name": "Michael Bi Mi"}], "abstract": "Robot agents built on vision-language models depend on both the planner and its harness: the tools, feedback, memory, and recovery logic around it. Can improvements to a large teacher's harness be carried into a compact agent with lower inference compute cost? We present TinyAstral, a two-stage approach. We first freeze an open-weight teacher and improve its harness through recursive self-improvement (RSI): frontier LLM agents read the teacher's trajectories and propose harness edits, and each edit is accepted only if it passes a paired statistical test. We then freeze the final harness, collect teacher trajectories with reasoning and failure recovery, and distill a small student that replaces the teacher in the same harness. On RoboTwin, harness evolution raises the teacher's success from 33.4% to 63.6%; the final 4B student reaches 62.8%. Across four harness versions, distilled 4B students score close to their corresponding teachers, and later versions yield stronger students, showing that harness evolution gains carry into compact models. The harness gains also transfer to other frozen planners without retraining. On identical episodes, the final 4B student outperforms open 4B agents, the open 27B harness-RSI model SenseNova-RoboRSI, and our own teacher model placed in three competitors'harnesses. It stays close to its teacher on all three training benchmarks, and on three benchmarks never used for RSI or training it scores close to a 552B planner running the same harness. On the measured deployment episodes, it uses about one fifth of its teacher's accelerator time."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a0dac219b30d06086741ab87aabc773d191a5342", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41350", "CorpusId": 295402474}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a0dac219b30d06086741ab87aabc773d191a5342", "title": "Operon-S1: a fast decision controller for long-horizon biomedical agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41350, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2525174799", "name": "Genoria AI Team Yuejie Hou"}, {"authorId": "2145217203", "name": "Yueyuxiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2269784284", "name": "Wei-Ting Tang"}, {"authorId": "25518906", "name": "Meng-Cheng Yang"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents now carry out long scientific work in biology, but their judgements about conventions, skills, errors, evidence and stopping are made inside the reasoning that does the work, where they cannot be measured, replaced or improved on their own. Here we present Operon-S1, a fast decision system, a System 1 for the agent, that separates judging from doing. A decision harness poses the agent's recurring judgements as closed questions at 13 decision points and acts only through fixed messages that never contain an answer, so any model that answers the questions can serve as the judge. Across seven life-science benchmarks, a local decision model took over these judgements in 0.6 s at the median for an in-loop request on CPU at no request cost, with no measurable change in the agent's score on five of six benchmarks, whereas the general language model that drives the agent, asked the same questions, accounted for 8.0% to 35.2% of task cost. Every decision is recorded with its consequence. Retraining on labels from task construction, verifiers or execution raised decision accuracy on every question in each of three trained versions, and a new version enters service only through a promotion gate. Task-level results in this study use the public checkpoint. The same closed decisions serve wet-laboratory protocol troubleshooting and the design of a germline target enrichment panel. Like the regulator of an operon, which switches genes on and off without making their products, Operon-S1 steers the agent without doing its work, and makes the agent's judgements a layer that can be measured, replaced and trained."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b19b0b08c501e09f00f772fb5dbf204b340f79c9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.39380", "CorpusId": 295373904}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b19b0b08c501e09f00f772fb5dbf204b340f79c9", "title": "Learning to Formulate: Agentic Hardware-Software Co-Design of AI Infrastructure through Progressive Optimization and Verification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39380, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515996173", "name": "Yi Ding"}, {"authorId": "2065825344", "name": "Li Shang"}], "abstract": "AI infrastructure, the chips and software that train and serve AI models, is designed in layers. Layers make the system manageable, but they also fix which choices an optimizer can consider. Gains that need the chip and its software to change together are excluded before optimization begins: the formulation leaves them out. Language-model agents make it possible to revisit these boundaries, but an agent's proposal cannot show by itself that a design is correct or optimal. We present Formulaic, a flow that divides the work. The agent writes the formulation, a mathematical solver optimizes within it, and verification and measurement check its assumptions and results. The agent refines the problem step by step, from the computation graph toward the chip. Each step is a template: a family of programs with the conditions under which they are correct. Wall relaxation searches the chip by pricing the hardware limits that bind the software. This paper takes a first step, through separate studies rather than one end-to-end run. On NVIDIA GB10, templates the agent wrote run complete Qwen3-8B layers $1.075\\times$ faster than SGLang. In a simulated Ventus GPU study, joint design cuts the worst-scenario cycles of Qwen2.5-0.5B to 0.428 of the reference, against 0.934 for software alone. On a five-step GEMM refinement chain, reusing proofs makes checking $2.51\\times$ faster."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0f78d0f9ce1d120a7dcef4015621fcb8b364ccab", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.40901", "CorpusId": 295402172}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0f78d0f9ce1d120a7dcef4015621fcb8b364ccab", "title": "TSFLab: A Self-Improving Agent-Agnostic Workbench for Time Series Forecasting Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40901, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2336050982", "name": "Chengao Shen"}, {"authorId": "2366026955", "name": "Yan-Jun Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2143856875", "name": "Haoxin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2519451673", "name": "Bo-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "90802789", "name": "Miles Ma"}, {"authorId": "2282141017", "name": "Dong-Jin Song"}, {"authorId": "2345188010", "name": "Hang-Hang Tong"}, {"authorId": "2364643457", "name": "Qing-Song Wen"}, {"authorId": "2303885774", "name": "Jing-Rui He"}, {"authorId": "2282536567", "name": "Jing-Chao Ni"}], "abstract": "As publications and products on time series forecasting (TSF) proliferate, researchers face a growing burden of analyzing existing work. First, not all publications release code. For instance, only about 20% of arXiv TSF papers have a code link in the surveyed period. Second, released code also requires manual maintenance. Thus, TSF researchers must constantly adapt or implement existing works. However, independent implementations can differ in experimental settings and are difficult to maintain. We therefore propose to use AI agents for benchmarking TSF research and even supporting autoresearch, e.g., developing new TSF models. Yet directly applying existing coding agents, such as Claude Code and Codex, is suboptimal: prohibitive costs, limited scalability to large research projects (e.g., benchmarking), and lack of self-maintenance. To address these challenges, we propose TSFLab, a self-improving agent-agnostic\"workbench\"that equips compatible coding agents with shared resources and tools for low-cost TSF research and continuous maintenance of research resources. Our experiments cover reproduction, benchmarking, and new method development. In reproduction and benchmarking, TSFLab improves task completion by 20-163% and cuts fresh tokens per completed task by 11-66% across baselines. A pilot study of agent-driven method development also shows improved forecasting accuracy via TSFLab, with 32.8-73.6% fewer fresh tokens and 9.2-68.8% less development time than the other settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3b1c21945586fe38f7b0c063cb1352b8b633fdc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38266", "CorpusId": 295372300}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3b1c21945586fe38f7b0c063cb1352b8b633fdc", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement in Physical AI: Mechanisms, Evidence, and Evaluation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38266, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2353440123", "name": "Xiao-Feng Han"}, {"authorId": "2524751956", "name": "Kai-Ze Luo"}, {"authorId": "2354260566", "name": "Zeng-Huang Fu"}, {"authorId": "2524803248", "name": "Zi-Chong Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2353852983", "name": "Zhe Feng"}, {"authorId": "1490749570", "name": "Changwei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2106213769", "name": "Rongtao Xu"}, {"authorId": "35965884", "name": "Wei-Liang Meng"}, {"authorId": "2386930994", "name": "Xiao-Peng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Embodied systems can update policies, world models, skills, and executable programs from deployment experience, allowing one interaction to benefit later tasks. When a system also revises and reuses its data-acquisition, feedback, or learning procedures, experience changes the process that produces capability, introducing recursive self-improvement. This paper surveys research in physical AI through the connection between persistent capability and its improvement process. We first explain the functional distinction between learner updates and improvers, then compare policy learning, imagined experience, memory and skill accumulation, and executable harnesses. These approaches connect to revisions of curricula, rewards, and research programs in different ways. Contact validity, recovery, retention, and resource costs determine whether the resulting gains accumulate during continued deployment. An L0-L3 rubric distinguishes task-local adaptation, persistent capability gain, inherited process revision, and verified improver gain. Representative benchmarks and within-study contrasts relate these distinctions to existing results. We further propose common-data and interactive comparisons that evaluate reference and revised improvers from shared learner starts, separating the use of experience from its acquisition. This perspective connects the diverse methods, physical operating conditions, and evaluation evidence, underpinning embodied RSI. A comprehensive list of studies in this survey is available at https://github.com/nkd-lkz/physical-ai-rsi-survey."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "085c7e19da1e386199bec4a67d3b0ff7e1e079c8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38568", "CorpusId": 295373274}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/085c7e19da1e386199bec4a67d3b0ff7e1e079c8", "title": "A-RSI: Adaptive Task Refresh for Zero-Data Multimodal Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38568, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2377553345", "name": "Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2117058608", "name": "You-Xing Li"}, {"authorId": "2303612781", "name": "Yue-Dong Tan"}, {"authorId": "2372904203", "name": "Zhi-Wei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2455609986", "name": "Zi-Hang Su"}, {"authorId": "2374431250", "name": "Han-Qi Feng"}, {"authorId": "2381352329", "name": "Shao-Jie Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2524781159", "name": "Li-Bin Liao"}, {"authorId": "2503735144", "name": "Zi-Hao Wan"}, {"authorId": "2524841564", "name": "Chen-Xu Wu"}, {"authorId": "2362300767", "name": "Cong Cao"}, {"authorId": "2042707137", "name": "Fang-Fang Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2268637935", "name": "Yan-Bing Liu"}, {"authorId": "2524791446", "name": "Zi-Heng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2381259557", "name": "Pei Fu"}, {"authorId": "1732855", "name": "R. Timofte"}, {"authorId": "2243310641", "name": "Zong-Wei Wu"}, {"authorId": "1429806753", "name": "N. Sebe"}, {"authorId": "2265973087", "name": "Barnab\u00e1s P\u00f3czos"}, {"authorId": "2257013742", "name": "Jian Luan"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement requires models not only to acquire useful training experience, but also to decide when their current experience has ceased to be useful. Existing methods often focus on what experience to generate next, while paying less attention to when fresh experience should be introduced. Many also construct training targets through self-consistency or majority voting, making supervision depend on the model's own uncertain predictions. We address these two questions separately: training dynamics determine when to acquire new experience, while the answers to new tasks remain independently checkable. A-RSI monitors reward variation and accuracy in training logs to refresh a task pool when its learning signal declines, without consulting validation or test scores. A static library of model-authored programs produces multimodal questions and derives their reference answers; we specify the task-generation contract, automatically verify chosen properties of the reward and refresh rules, and separately audit the generated answers. The training loop uses no external training examples or human-written answer labels. On the primary eleven-benchmark suite, A-RSI raises the performance average from 57.77 to 61.05 with 800 fresh tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "31fcb9ec2bee6868189d28d205680d78c2a06bfd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38392", "CorpusId": 295373262}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/31fcb9ec2bee6868189d28d205680d78c2a06bfd", "title": "Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38392, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2424570721", "name": "Hau-Sing So"}, {"authorId": "2187282034", "name": "Dai-Xian Liu"}, {"authorId": "2342512497", "name": "Wei-Cai Long"}], "abstract": "Self-improving LLM agents edit their own harness, the prompts and rules around a fixed model, and keep an edit only if an acceptance gate scores it higher on a batch of tasks. Such a gate sees what the harness does under the current model, not what it would still prevent if one safeguard failed. When safeguards overlap, deleting one is masked: the others still block the harm, so the gate decides on task success alone and redundancy erodes unseen. We measure harm for every subset of four written safeguards in two agent environments and run 411 improvement loops under ten gate rules, mostly as controlled deletion experiments, plus three runs of the GEPA optimizer. A deletion behind another working safeguard adds almost no observable harm. A paired significance test blocks deletions that pass by chance but not deletions that genuinely raise task success, and a larger evaluation batch lets it delete cheaper safeguards: at the same cost, 20-task batches kept every harness protected while 40-task batches lost all six. Large harm audits prevent exposure but not the loss of depth, which turns into harm when the harness is reused with a weaker model. GEPA kept every safety rule verbatim, yet two of its three final harnesses misused credentials under a weaker model. A statistical model frozen before the runs predicts where each gate's protection breaks as safeguard cost and batch size change, and freezing safety text or auditing one-layer ablations preserves depth."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36660", "CorpusId": 294524268}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "title": "The View from Nowhere: Population-Centered Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36660, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521792727", "name": "Shuo Yan"}, {"authorId": "2521582669", "name": "Ning-Ning Hou"}, {"authorId": "2326915783", "name": "Hao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2374041291", "name": "Hao-Chen Pei"}, {"authorId": "2520331106", "name": "Shang Gao"}], "abstract": "Self-improving AI systems can revise the designs that govern their own behavior. Yet existing approaches center improvement on selected parent agents and their evolutionary experience, leaving the broader population largely underused. We identify a substantial gap between population coverage (i.e., the tasks solved by at least one evolved variant) and the performance of the best individual agent. This gap shows that useful capabilities and diagnostic evidence emerge across the population without being consolidated into its leading agents. These observations motivate the view from nowhere, a principle for agent evolution: an agent should be improved by interpreting its behavior through population-wide patterns of success, failure, and prior self-modification. We instantiate this principle as Population-Centered Evolution (PCE). PCE distils task records and modification histories from the population into a population block, which captures recurring failures, complementary successes, and the effects of previously attempted modifications to guide improvements to a selected parent's harness. Under matched models, initial harnesses, and evolution schedules, PCE achieves 72.1% success on SWE-bench Verified and 96.7% on Polyglot, improving over a strong group-evolution baseline by 3.2 and 5.9 percentage points. In our evaluation, PCE reduces the gap between population coverage and best-agent performance by one-third on SWE-bench and closes it on Polyglot. These results suggest that treating the population as evidence, not merely as candidates for selection, provides a complementary principle for agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4cdcda26db4443384bb6848b21f4e1e44e261288", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36578", "CorpusId": 294524494}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4cdcda26db4443384bb6848b21f4e1e44e261288", "title": "AudioRSI: Recursively Self-Improving Audio Reasoners", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36578, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2344928100", "name": "Jia-Jun Fan"}, {"authorId": "2154762054", "name": "Roger Ren"}, {"authorId": "2366176848", "name": "Jing-Yuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2372652749", "name": "R. Pandey"}, {"authorId": "2776289", "name": "Prashanth Gurunath Shivakumar"}, {"authorId": "36881920", "name": "Ankur Gandhe"}, {"authorId": "2112579196", "name": "Yi-Le Gu"}, {"authorId": "2324070404", "name": "Ge Liu"}, {"authorId": "2575459", "name": "I. Bulyko"}], "abstract": "Reinforcement learning has made Audio Large Language Models (Audio LLMs) better reasoners, but the reward that drives it still comes from outside the model: a final-answer check, a hand-crafted process score, or an external LLM judge that stays fixed while the policy improves. We show that an Audio LLM can supply this reward itself, and can do so recursively: each improved model becomes the critic that trains its successor, so the training signal improves along with the model. We introduce AudioRSI (Recursively Self-Improving Audio Reasoners), a reinforcement learning framework in which a single model plays two roles: as an actor it generates reasoning paths, and as a critic it compares them pairwise, turning its own judgments into a process-level reward. The critic is periodically replaced by the current policy, which closes the recursive loop. Because the critic hears the same recording as the actor, it can reject reasoning that cites acoustic evidence the audio does not contain, rather than reward reasoning that merely sounds plausible. The recursion is what sustains progress: under a frozen external judge, out-of-distribution accuracy plateaus during training, while a critic refreshed from the improving policy keeps it rising. Across four out-of-distribution benchmarks, all disjoint from the AVQA training distribution, AudioRSI outperforms strong baselines including Audio Flamingo 3 and Gemini 2.5 Pro on MMAU test-mini, ranks first on MMSU, achieves a clear lead on the challenging Multi-Audio split of MMAU-Pro, and leads the 7B open-source tier on MMAR among methods without external reward models. With no reward specifying how to reason, AudioRSI also develops structured reasoning behaviors (logical reasoning, perception-aware analysis, and reduced hallucination), corroborated by an external LLM-as-Judge evaluation and a keyword-based behavior analysis."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eb897335e77ce50c6adaee19c8febd66b7c49dc3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.37046", "CorpusId": 294621734}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb897335e77ce50c6adaee19c8febd66b7c49dc3", "title": "Learning to Code the World: Harness Evolution for Compositional 3D Reconstruction", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37046, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2399932302", "name": "Hao-Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2283193527", "name": "Chen-Guo Lin"}, {"authorId": "2521918445", "name": "Rui-Ze Du"}, {"authorId": "2283135775", "name": "Ya-Dong Mu"}], "abstract": "Reconstructing editable and compositional 3D scenes from a real image requires jointly reasoning about geometry, object structure, and spatial relationships. We introduce SceneEvolve, a framework that represents 3D scenes as executable code and improves reconstruction by evolving the harness around a small open-source agent. SceneEvolve combines SceneAct, a compositional semantic action interface built on parameterized primitives, hierarchical composition, and semantic spatial relations, with SceneHarness-RSI, which leverages a stronger teacher agent to iteratively evolve the harness using feedback from development-set reconstructions. Throughout harness evolution, the model parameters, action semantics, and execution backend remain fixed. The resulting harness provides a reusable reconstruction procedure that enables the small open-source model to reconstruct unseen scenes without parameter updates or teacher assistance. Across two in-domain datasets and one out-of-domain dataset, SceneEvolve with the comparatively compact Qwen3.8-27B model improves all four evaluated reconstruction metrics while reducing mean inference time by 47.0% relative to the base model. More notably, despite relying on a substantially smaller model, SceneEvolve achieves 32.3% higher reconstruction quality than the large proprietary GPT-5.6-Sol model, while reducing mean equivalent deployment cost by 59.8%. Furthermore, the harness evolved using Qwen3.8-27B also generalizes to Kimi-K3, achieving comparable reconstruction performance to GPT-6-Astra. The project page is https://haoyu-agent.github.io/SceneEvolve."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4b65400e42f49dafe5294baa8fde8916a195c766", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35579", "CorpusId": 294367011}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4b65400e42f49dafe5294baa8fde8916a195c766", "title": "Infinity Machine: a self-improving AI agent for research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35579, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "30861801", "name": "H. Ganesan"}], "abstract": "We present the Infinity Machine, a self-improving AI agent that we use for research on problems whose answers aprogram or data can check. Each candidate solution is produced by a full tool-using agent that works for up to an hour in a fresh sandbox; proposers see only a public score, and a private split decides what counts. The harness itself(prompts, tools, skills, playbook, meta agent) is an editable genome, changed only through a kernel-enforced gate with held-out and cost checks. An autonomous mode goes from a plain-text goal to a verified report with no human step: an agent picks an open problem and writes its verifier, the kernel calibrates it, an adversarial agent audits it, and an agent that never sees the verifier re-checks the result. Across fourteen problems in six days, for about $180 of model usage, the system produced four results beyond the published state of the art on catalogued open problems in quantum information: a certified depolarizing-channel threshold point p = 0.064966, a qutrit positivity frontier of 0.2910672, a 44-gate depth-6 Golay state preparation, and a stabilizer-rank bound chi(T^7)<= 10. It also cut SABRE qubit-routing overhead from 68,559 to 12,087 added gates. In hardware, it cut the energy per MAC of an open 8x8 INT8 systolic tile by 77.7% through a full open RTL-to-GDS flow, bit-exact. Three fully autonomous runs ended in honestties with public records; one audit caught a stale record, and one agent-written score pointing the wrong way went unnoticed. We report the failures as carefully: the one self-derived harness generation was rejected on cost, proposers gamed lenient scorers four times, and operator changes were decisive for several results."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "325d67d435744343acf9e961a0576d4c8b119c74", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35434", "CorpusId": 294365951}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/325d67d435744343acf9e961a0576d4c8b119c74", "title": "Skill$^{\\infty}$: Recursive Self-Improvement for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35434, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2269473442", "name": "Wang-Cheng Tao"}, {"authorId": "2000364673", "name": "Zhang-Lu Yan"}, {"authorId": "2360250854", "name": "Han Wu"}, {"authorId": "2268429434", "name": "Weng-Fai Wong"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI), in which a system also improves the way it improves, is a long-standing aspiration on the path to AGI. Language agents increasingly store procedural knowledge in reusable skills. Optimizing skills offers a direct way to achieve RSI, because the optimization procedure itself can be encoded as a skill. Existing skill optimizers, however, follow a hand-designed procedure. Even methods that evolve a meta-skill confine it to a fixed pipeline and relearn it for each task. We present Skill$^{\\infty}$, which makes skill optimization recursively self-improving: a meta agent follows a meta-skill to optimize task skills and optimizes the meta-skill itself using the same procedure. The optimization is agent-led. The meta agent diagnoses execution traces, proposes candidates, requests independent reviews, and allocates the search budget. The meta-skill defines these steps, allowing self-improvement to rewrite them. This meta-skill is also decoupled from task skills, refined on multiple skill optimization tasks and then reused to optimize task skills across diverse domains. As in deployment, skills at both levels are standard packages loaded through native skill discovery. On 83 SkillsBench tasks, Skill$^{\\infty}$ reaches a pass rate of 60.7%, compared with 56.4% for human-curated skills and 54.5% for SkillEvolver."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "beebc1f82d57a7188e17bf6e2afd9a4f67c4e0b6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32253", "CorpusId": 293935928}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/beebc1f82d57a7188e17bf6e2afd9a4f67c4e0b6", "title": "RSIHub: A Composable Framework for Agent-Evolution Methods", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32253, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2374373758", "name": "Si-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent-evolution methods use execution feedback to improve prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration, but their implementations often rebuild the same experimental machinery. We present RSIHub, an open-source framework for building, comparing, and extending these methods on shared infrastructure. Recipes compose reusable operators and declare mutable resources; RSIHub supplies execution, evaluation, recovery, and lineage. Researchers can focus on evolution policy while evaluation stays outside the mutable surface and results remain tied to candidates and evaluation conditions. We instantiate AHE, Hyperagents, A-Evolve, and GEPA without modifying the framework core, keeping method-specific logic in recipes and operators. We evaluate them with MiniSWE and Codex on Terminal Bench 2 and $\\tau^3$ Banking under fixed model and evaluator configurations. The largest observed overall pass@1 gain is 13.5 percentage points, while method rankings vary across settings and optimization gains do not always transfer to held-out tasks. RSIHub provides a common protocol for comparing evolution policies, inspectable candidate histories, and reusable components for developing new methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "438c64ee68ca929c3bc37a9124972f8caf5fdebb", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32120", "CorpusId": 293935947}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/438c64ee68ca929c3bc37a9124972f8caf5fdebb", "title": "ImageRSI: Verification-Driven Recursive Self-Improvement for Agentic Image Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32120, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2319869433", "name": "Shuo Han"}, {"authorId": "2517892880", "name": "Zhi-Lin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2517305138", "name": "Peng Ling"}, {"authorId": "2517305812", "name": "Wen-Jia Gong"}, {"authorId": "2517555416", "name": "Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2366578182", "name": "Guan-Jun Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2112823208", "name": "Yukun Cao"}, {"authorId": "2279249222", "name": "Xi-Ke Xie"}], "abstract": "Complex text-to-image requests remain difficult to satisfy reliably. Verifying and repairing an image can fix an individual output, but a successful repair does not necessarily carry over to other requests. We present ImageRSI, a framework that keeps the image generator frozen and combines three components: five-dimensional verification and repair within each task, an experience library shared across tasks, and offline updating of the judge. Candidate experiences enter the library only after paired A/B validation, and the judge is updated with supervised fine-tuning and GRPO on accumulated image-judgment records and evaluated before reuse. We also introduce ImageReq-Bench, a benchmark of 961 requests built around hard cases in real-world image-generation requests, each paired with an evaluation checklist. ImageRSI achieves a Checklist Strict Accuracy (CSA) of 52.6% on MindBench (with Nano Banana Pro as the generator) and 65.66% on ImageReq-Bench, and we compare system outcomes across judge-training configurations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32026", "CorpusId": 293935942}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "title": "RSI-Opt: Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents For Model Training", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32026, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2195352476", "name": "Bing-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2308106471", "name": "Yi-Ge Sun"}, {"authorId": "2364827101", "name": "Guang Lin"}], "abstract": "Conventional neural-network training typically follows a predefined optimization recipe, motivating a LLM agent that monitors training dynamics and adjusts optimizer settings as training progresses, but a fixed agent policy does not accumulate experience across runs. We introduce RSI-Opt, a plug-and-play Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents which evolves the knowledge that guides this process: a prompt, a playbook of procedures, a library of tested optimizer code, and a decision cadence. A meta-agent rewrites this policy using evidence from earlier runs, and an archive retains evaluated policies for further evolution. Across 5 generations on three training tasks, the meta-agents develop reusable procedures and code and conduct their own CPU proxy experiments. RSI-Opt substantially improves optimizer-agent performance, with representative raw-space summary $z$-scores increasing from $+10.48$ to $+14.33$ on the evolution suite with Opus 5.5 and from $-0.36$ to $+3.11$ on held-out tasks with GPT-5.5 through cross-model policy transfer. On the Burgers PDE, it reduces mean relative $L_2$ error by approximately 2.7-fold with Opus 5.5 and 8.8-fold with GPT-6.1 over the fixed-prompt agent baseline, although PDE transfer varies across LLMs and tasks. Controlled comparisons show that the evolved policy combines reusable optimization knowledge with adaptation to each task: initial decisions capture most observed gains on seen tasks, while continued adaptation improves outcomes on unseen tasks. These findings demonstrate how procedures and executable tools accumulated across training runs can improve optimizer-agent performance and support transfer to scientific machine learning."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32813", "CorpusId": 293940253}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "title": "HarnessMM: Recursive Self-Improvement for Harness Token Efficiency", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2345305718", "name": "Yu-Hua Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2509359032", "name": "Chang-Feng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2390234953", "name": "Fei-Fei Gao"}, {"authorId": "2299119265", "name": "Li Zhao"}], "abstract": "HarnessMinusMinus (HarnessMM) is a research project on improving the efficiency of agent harnesses, the software coordinating model reasoning, tool execution, and feedback. This technical report consolidates its shared framework, implementations, and empirical studies across coding, tool-based, and visual-agent settings. Agent harnesses often invoke reasoning after each tool result, even when earlier calls have already determined subsequent actions. HarnessMM addresses this overhead through guarded decision reuse and recursive self-improvement (RSI). RSI refines author prompts, context settings, and adapter rules using training feedback, with validation selecting deployment configurations. At inference time, a controller reuses resolved decisions while keeping actor weights fixed. Executable Decision Graphs encode actions, arguments, dependencies, and guards. Guarded Decision Reuse activates eligible decisions from observed feedback, while Certified Callback Substitution executes them through native tool interfaces, replacing redundant reasoning callbacks. The original actor resumes when new reasoning is required or reuse conditions are unmet. On HumanEval, HarnessMM preserves Codex success at 90.82\\% while reducing tokens per task from 66.72k to 47.02k (29.54\\% saving). For DeepSeek Harness, success improves from 71.43\\% to 85.71\\% while tokens decrease from 96.28k to 11.62k (87.93\\% saving). An MBPP ablation shows 26.91\\% lower cost than single-use graphs and 19.52\\% lower cost than retained callbacks. The report also summarizes the HarnessMM-V visual-agent study and documents optimization overhead and execution time where measured. Together, these studies investigate guarded decision reuse across diverse agent harness settings. Code is available at https://github.com/YuhuaJiang2002/HarnessMM."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31034", "CorpusId": 293894206}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "title": "CANNBot-CAKE: Collaborative Agentic Kernel Evolution for Ascend NPU", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31034, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2452537473", "name": "Deng Pan"}, {"authorId": "2516983810", "name": "Xue-Jian Gao"}, {"authorId": "2290318708", "name": "Jun-Yi Fan"}, {"authorId": "2452538545", "name": "Bin Du"}, {"authorId": "2479282931", "name": "Jia-Sheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2454210612", "name": "Xin-Xian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2516888290", "name": "Nok-Yan Chang"}, {"authorId": "2504354370", "name": "Xinlin Li"}, {"authorId": "2383066909", "name": "Ling-Chao Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383854073", "name": "Jun Li"}, {"authorId": "2383326945", "name": "Yu-Wei Fan"}], "abstract": "Efficient custom operators are essential for modern deep learning workloads, yet developing high-performance kernels for Ascend NPUs remains slow, expert-intensive, and difficult to automate. Although AI agents now approach expert performance on GPU kernel benchmarks, current models remain less capable of generating AscendC kernels for NPUs, potentially reflecting the relative scarcity of relevant training data. We thus present CAKE, a CANN-native system for deployable custom operators that combines adaptive multi-DSL routing, separate Generation and Evolution modes, and reviewed knowledge reuse through CAKE Book and a Self-Evolving Agent. On 53 CANN-Bench v1.1 operators on Ascend 950PR, CAKE's aggregated results achieve $100\\%$ case pass rate on the standard set (1,060/1,060) and $98.28\\%$ on the hidden set (4,167/4,240), with an overall mean score of 78.63 out of 100 and an average performance speedup of $1.334\\times$ over the baseline. Evolution then pushes correct operators further: EngramGateFusion cuts aggregate device time by $46.6\\%$ over 20 rounds and roughly halves the mean roofline gap. These results offer a practical path toward closing Ascend's AI-for-kernel capability gap."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5c1de74ecd644cd617f56bed685607c2491651bd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29121", "CorpusId": 293844964}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5c1de74ecd644cd617f56bed685607c2491651bd", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement on a Budget: Scoring Code Edits Before They Run", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29121, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "1402017423", "name": "Erfan Darzi"}, {"authorId": "2302641458", "name": "Sree Bhargavi Balija"}, {"authorId": "2510025844", "name": "Sheng-Feng He"}], "abstract": "A coding agent that rewrites its own scaffold has to choose which proposed edit to expand before that edit has been evaluated, and the search then has to return one agent. Each run fits one linear readout on the parent's steering-split solve rate and eight coordinates of the proposed diff, from 96 scaffold edits labeled by four evaluations of a 16-instance fit split, and then freezes the coefficients. HumanEval+ reserves 48 instances as the steering split and Polyglot reserves 64. That split scores parents and keeps an archive of four nodes. Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection draws a parent by Thompson sampling on the steering counts of nodes that have already been evaluated, and it expands the child that parent proposes. Every method then returns the earliest evaluated node whose steering-split solve count is maximal. The tables report the held-out score of that node on the other 100 HumanEval+ instances and the other 145 Polyglot instances, next to the root scaffold and the calibration edit with the highest fit-split label. Twelve runs of GPT-4o return a node at 87.0% on held-out HumanEval+, against 84.2% for the parent-rate ablation. On that split Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection returns 85.1%, the root is at 81.2%, and the calibration-best edit is at 82.8%. On the same split Claude Sonnet 4.5 returns 86.6%, beside 86.5% for Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection, 85.3% for the parent-rate ablation, and 85.1% for the calibration-best edit."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e3513be2c876547d70de2a3a168cebbca14b50b5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29641", "CorpusId": 293857274}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e3513be2c876547d70de2a3a168cebbca14b50b5", "title": "Recursive Code Evolution for Task-Oriented Semantic Communications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29641, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering", "Mathematics"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2402867389", "name": "Yan Gong"}, {"authorId": "2238954545", "name": "Z. Chu"}, {"authorId": "2468946065", "name": "Wei-Ran Chai"}, {"authorId": "2264836679", "name": "Hari Mohan Pandey"}, {"authorId": "151376244", "name": "Jingrui Hou"}, {"authorId": "46324827", "name": "Miao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2110454776", "name": "Yuan-Jian Li"}, {"authorId": "2293392561", "name": "Pei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Semantic communication jointly optimizes inference accuracy, channel use, and computation, yet the underlying communication program is typically specified before training. This paper presents ComRSI (Communications-Oriented Recursive Self-Improvement), a framework for recursively evolving that program through large-language-model-assisted code edits. An agent modifies the encoder, channel adapters, decoder, loss, and training policy; a protected evaluator screens candidates under prescribed budgets and resource constraints. Accepted code and textual feedback inform subsequent proposals, while candidate weights are trained from scratch. In the original ten-generation CIFAR-100 lineage, eight accepted revisions produce an attention-pooled code achieving 74.3% test accuracy over an Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) channel at 32 complex symbols per image, compared with 67.2% at 256 symbols for the initial design. The program also improves fading-channel accuracy and transfers to Tiny-ImageNet after retraining, where a separate ComRSI search demonstrates further recursive improvements. A repeat study with ten searches per method compares ComRSI against non-recursive agents with and without textual history. Mean final search scores balancing accuracy, transmission rate, and computational cost are 0.7587, 0.7376, and 0.7089, respectively; the mean numbers of accepted revisions per search are 6.0, 5.0, and 2.1. The advantage over the memoryless agent is supported by a two-sided rank test, whereas the difference from the history-equipped agent remains statistically inconclusive at this sample size. Archived proposals show that pooling can yield a large improvement, while retained history supports subsequent refinement. These results support recursive program optimization within the evaluated setting and expose its remaining rate-robustness and compute-placement trade-offs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "275264328ee9b1f15ad427237b6219337be722f6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30116", "CorpusId": 293857099}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/275264328ee9b1f15ad427237b6219337be722f6", "title": "Environment Duels: A Microcosm of LLM Co-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30116, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2322650890", "name": "Shi-Bo Jin"}, {"authorId": "2461897256", "name": "Zhi-Qiu Xu"}, {"authorId": "2516811808", "name": "Yi-Ling Zou"}, {"authorId": "2516603637", "name": "Shang-Yu Gong"}, {"authorId": "2258375157", "name": "Mayur Naik"}], "abstract": "Through a tangle of real-world interactions, from synthetic training pipelines to data shared on the open web, frontier LLMs absorb what their predecessors and competitors create. How well does each model play its role in this ecosystem, as a creator and as a solver? We introduce Environment Duels, a dynamic agentic benchmark that evaluates both capabilities on interactive environments. Each model writes environments, each with a privileged hint, aiming for tasks that capable solvers fail without the hint and pass with it. Across nine frontier LLMs, solving and authoring prove to be distinct skills: the strongest solvers are not the strongest authors, while smaller models learn more from how others played and write the hardest environments and the most useful hints. Beyond the scores, the pairwise interactions form a microcosm of how models affect one another. Head-to-head outcomes are transitive and so yield a stable ranking; independent authors converge on the same designs; and in a reinforcement-learning study with Qwen3.8-27B, the learner's hint lift on an author's environments tracks how much training on those environments improves it on held-out reasoning and agentic benchmarks, whether the environments are its own or another model's. Each of these patterns has a counterpart in how models shape one another outside the duel. By staging these interactions under controlled conditions, Environment Duels offers a window onto how LLMs teach, challenge and learn from one another. Code is available at https://github.com/LLM-Duels/EnvDuels."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30499", "CorpusId": 293894326}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "title": "Can LLMs Improve Their Own Harness? A Controlled Study on ARC-AGI-3", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30499, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291593072", "name": "Jens Lehmann"}, {"authorId": "2327466746", "name": "Sahar Vahdati"}], "abstract": "A generally intelligent system acquires new skills efficiently without requiring humans to redesign its software scaffold for every new task. Yet language models rarely act alone. A harness is a symbolic program that orchestrates calls to the model, handling perception, memory, tool use, recovery, and inference allocation on the model's behalf. ARC-AGI-3 is an interactive benchmark in which agents must explore unfamiliar environments, figure out their goals on the fly, and keep learning as they go. On it, human-steered systems already report ceiling scores across the public games, so we know effective designs exist. We ask whether frontier models can build and improve such a harness on their own. Through a fixed coding bootstrap, each model constructs a complete task harness, acts through that harness on five development games, and revises the entire codebase twice. Every version is evaluated on twenty games that never influence adaptation. We measure task performance with Relative Human Action Efficiency (RHAE), which combines level completion with action efficiency relative to human baselines, and record dollar-denominated inference cost as a practical resource measure. Across 27 lineages, harness performance ranges from zero to near-ceiling scores. Two revisions raise mean held-out RHAE by only 2.80 points (95% interval [-1.44,7.35]). Development gains overstate held-out improvement, and reductions in inference cost can come at the expense of task performance. Human-steered systems demonstrate further headroom. These findings reveal a gap between autonomous harness construction and reliable adaptation under this two-revision protocol: even strong task performance does not ensure that subsequent revisions generalize or preserve existing capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27610", "CorpusId": 293809462}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "title": "Auditing the Product, Not the Recursion: The Evaluation Evidence Behind Recursive Self-Improvement Claims", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27610, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2255142697", "name": "Zi-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "36737152", "name": "Di Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2233239462", "name": "L. Zheng"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) promises AI systems, such as self-evolving LLM agents, that become better at producing further improvements. Evaluating this promise requires separating product claims about the final system from process claims about the improvement process itself. Existing surveys specify the evidence these claims require, but how often studies report that evidence remains unmeasured. We examine this question through full-text coding of 114 eligible studies in a reproducible arXiv search frame frozen in October 2026, including 90 that describe their own systems as RSI. The codebook links claims to evaluation designs. Independent human coding, third-reader adjudication, and a separate blind coding of improver comparisons assess coding reliability. Among the 90 RSI studies, 90% report product evaluation beyond development tasks under a broad criterion that includes partial or unstated separation, while 60% report evaluation on tasks unused for selection. Only 22% audit two or more reruns of the full loop. In a human-coded random sample, the RSI studies show the same contrast: 45% evaluate on tasks unused for selection and 14% audit two or more reruns. Of the 26 studies claiming a better improvement procedure, 23 report a related comparison, but only 4 compare earlier and later procedures from identical starting artifacts at a matched budget. Across these results and a comparison set of established works, evidence for repeatability and improved procedures remains less developed than product evaluation. Three propositions explain how selection effects, shared evaluator errors, and changes in system state limit what product evaluations can establish. Together, the findings support a claim-indexed reporting standard that connects each claim to the evaluation design needed to test it."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28785", "CorpusId": 293845201}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "title": "Measure First, Then Allocate: Calibrated Rollout Budgets for MCP Agents in Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28785, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2263121822", "name": "Hieu Tran"}, {"authorId": "1576489304", "name": "Zonghai Yao"}, {"authorId": "2335844854", "name": "Sunjae Kwon"}, {"authorId": "2261455807", "name": "Hong Yu"}], "abstract": "Training tool-using language models requires costly interaction, yet identical rewards within a rollout group provide no group-relative learning signal. We use reward disagreement to guide harness evaluation and the allocation of rollouts for policy training in Model Context Protocol (MCP) environments. We first introduce Discord, which tests harness edits using exact paired inference and decision-preserving early stopping: only disagreeing paired outcomes favor one harness over another. This insight motivates prospective allocation in BRACE (Bank-calibrated Rollout Allocation under Correlated Episodes). BRACE combines historical task evidence, uncertainty in task success rates, and a correlated reward model to forecast mixed-reward groups before allocating generation budget. Controlled harness experiments favor Discord, while live MCP experiments expose a retention-cost trade-off. At three Qwen3-VL model scales, BRACE exceeds the strongest plotted baseline peaks with fewer new training rollouts; historical-bank collection is counted separately. It also leads the compared methods on BFCL and NESTFUL without further training. Ablations probe the bank, estimator, and allocation score; prospective evaluation checks forecast calibration. These results support forecasting reward contrast before generation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9bd5e1f8e03de0a30b1853c6fda3299586dd89b1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03674", "CorpusId": 295506503}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9bd5e1f8e03de0a30b1853c6fda3299586dd89b1", "title": "ReVi: Recursive Recap Video Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03674, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505774015", "name": "Kyrie You Wu"}, {"authorId": "2245533308", "name": "Chao-Zhuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2367088216", "name": "Song-Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2163940136", "name": "Xue-Yang Feng"}, {"authorId": "2441627825", "name": "Ziluowen Luo"}, {"authorId": "2367092520", "name": "Chen-Xu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2405096448", "name": "Ji Qi"}, {"authorId": "2526388106", "name": "Jin-Fu Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2292634820", "name": "Li-Tian Zhang"}], "abstract": "Novels tell rich stories, but their textual form cannot directly present the sights and sounds of the worlds they describe. Film adaptations offer an audiovisual experience but require substantial production effort and viewing time; film recaps are shorter but depend on existing footage. Neither route directly provides a concise audiovisual account of a novel. We therefore study novel-to-recap video generation: producing a self-contained, source-grounded audiovisual retelling directly from a novel within a fixed viewing-time budget. This task requires compressing the story while preserving causal connections, allocating sufficient time to retained events, and aligning narration with newly generated visuals. We propose ReVi, a dual-loop multi-agent framework guided by edit first, generate second. Its Inner Loop plans story content, narration time, and corresponding shots before video generation. Across novels, its Outer Loop performs recursive self-improvement by revising both the editing policy and the meta policy that governs its revision. Experiments on two evaluation sets show that ReVi improves recap quality and duration compliance over representative baselines, with human ratings and multi-round improvement results providing further support."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1bd1a5b2b11be0806caa5e1d7fdf85bba38074ab", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33046", "CorpusId": 294227948}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1bd1a5b2b11be0806caa5e1d7fdf85bba38074ab", "title": "Organon : From Evolutionary Search to Recursive Self-Improving Agents for Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33046, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2334737967", "name": "C. Ahmed"}, {"authorId": "2521084905", "name": "Zhi-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2467333430", "name": "Kun Mao"}, {"authorId": "2506677818", "name": "Yong-He Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2376390381", "name": "Zi-Rui Zhou"}], "abstract": "Scientific discovery does not follow a single fixed methodology: research methods vary across domains and evolve through experimentation as investigations unfold. Although LLM-guided evolutionary search has advanced automated discovery, existing frameworks typically rely on fixed search procedures or restrict adaptation to predefined components, limiting their ability to address diverse research problems and adapt to challenges that emerge at runtime. We introduce Organon, a recursively self-improving agent for scientific discovery. During execution, a single persistent agent analyzes its accumulated experience and autonomously revises its own search strategies, population management, diagnostic tools, memory, and worker coordination. These revisions take effect at runtime, allowing the agent to continuously adapt its approach as research progresses. Importantly, the agent can also diagnose and correct flaws in its own adaptation procedures, enabling recursive improvement of the mechanisms that guide its future self-improvement. We evaluate Organon across 21 computational discovery tasks spanning algorithm design, GPU kernel optimization, mathematical construction, and systems optimization. On ALE-Bench Lite, Organon achieves the best performance among competing search frameworks on all 10 tasks, improving mean held-out performance by 20.5% over EvoX. On KernelBench, it achieves the best results on three of four workloads, with speedups of up to 1.67x over the strongest competing method. Analysis of the search trajectories further reveals how the agent develops task-specific search strategies, builds new diagnostic capabilities, and corrects or reverses its own earlier revisions. We implement Organon using DeepSeek Harness and Cordis's spatiotemporal composability. Code is available at https://github.com/cheikh025/Organon."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "df4d31b3b58fd6e94c396dce6de6b129ad6312ca", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23470", "CorpusId": 293545984}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/df4d31b3b58fd6e94c396dce6de6b129ad6312ca", "title": "QD-Rec: Open-Ended Evolution of Recommendations via Quality-Diversity", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23470, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2069409929", "name": "Takashi Ishida"}, {"authorId": "2099390928", "name": "Taro Makino"}, {"authorId": "2310230616", "name": "Issa Sugiura"}], "abstract": "Open-ended search combined with foundation models has produced advances such as faster algorithms, new results on open mathematical problems, and self-improving agents. We extend this paradigm to recommendation for decision-making, focusing on decisions where the set of possible choices is fixed but the reasons for choosing them are not. In high-stakes decisions, e.g., which company to acquire or which customer to target, comparing diverse, well-reasoned options can be more useful than a single ranked list. We formalize open-ended recommendation as building a diverse, high-quality set of recommendations, each an item together with a hypothesis, a natural-language reason why the item fits the needs of a decision maker. We propose QD-Rec, a quality--diversity method that selects items with a contextual bandit and generates hypotheses with a foundation model from earlier ones kept in an archive as stepping stones. A new recommendation enters the archive if it is novel or better than its nearest neighbor. Across M&A, customer targeting, and paper and book recommendation, QD-Rec gives the highest mean quality and builds the largest archive in most settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8b1750757ace10315d76ddf1c595bb3d2aa72eef", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21170", "CorpusId": 292982492}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8b1750757ace10315d76ddf1c595bb3d2aa72eef", "title": "MemScope: Evolving Agent Memory Architectures through Localized Code Repair", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21170, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2244622090", "name": "Miao Su"}, {"authorId": "2265608871", "name": "Yu-Can Guo"}, {"authorId": "46947005", "name": "Zi-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2075398318", "name": "Long Bai"}, {"authorId": "1560391730", "name": "Zhong-Ni Hou"}, {"authorId": "2149111400", "name": "Xiao-Long Jin"}, {"authorId": "70414094", "name": "Jia-Feng Guo"}], "abstract": "Agent memory architectures define how LLM agents store and reuse experience across tasks, but they are typically fixed after deployment, limiting their adaptability across task types. Recent self-evolving approaches instead adapt these memory architectures using execution feedback, typically represented as evaluation scores or compressed summaries. However, such coarse-grained feedback is insufficient for precise failure localization in current agent systems, where long-horizon task execution involves multiple interacting components. Without a precise failure location, existing approaches lack a clear code-level target for modification, leaving the update scope weakly constrained and often leading to large-scale rewrites of an otherwise capable memory architecture. We propose MemScope, a framework for evidence-grounded, localized evolution of an existing memory architecture. Its tool-using Patch Agent jointly inspects raw execution traces, runtime memory state, and current architecture code to identify repair targets and apply bounded, in-place edits under provider-identity and memory-compatibility constraints. On GAIA, the trace-and-memory inspection variant achieves 86.67% accuracy, compared with 70.00% for trace-only and 63.33% for summary-only feedback. Under xBench tasks, the evolved memory architecture improves over its original version from 49 to 55 correct answers with DeepSeek V3.2 and from 50 to 53 with Kimi K2.7 Code. On disjoint ALFWorld unseen tasks, success rises from 50.75% to 71.64%. Across archived architecture-snapshot transitions, the median code-change ratio is 4.4%. These results support fine-grained execution evidence as a basis for localized, in-place memory-architecture evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d017d7888d16683c5109c744494f88110b55c050", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21887", "CorpusId": 293003845}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d017d7888d16683c5109c744494f88110b55c050", "title": "MetaShare: Failure-Aware Collective Self-Improvement for Open-Ended Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21887, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2049035448", "name": "Vishal Bhutani"}, {"authorId": "2003680911", "name": "Prasang Gupta"}, {"authorId": "2444865616", "name": "Sumanth Chundru"}, {"authorId": "2054437310", "name": "K. Paul"}], "abstract": "Humans often learn fastest not by repeating success, but by remembering which failures not to repeat. Open-ended self-improving agents can share experience across lineages, as in GEA, or edit their own improvement mechanisms, as in DGM-Hyperagents, yet failed improvement attempts are rarely retained as reusable cross-branch knowledge, and search can collapse onto redundant strategies. We introduce MetaShare, a failure-aware framework that records successful and unsuccessful agent modifications together with signed performance deltas. Before each generation, MetaShare retrieves diverse high-performing strategies alongside high-confidence failures from other lineages, letting agents reuse effective mechanisms while avoiding known dead ends. A task-blind auditor periodically injects absent or underrepresented mechanism categories, while embedding-based de-ranking limits redundancy during retrieval. Multiple meta-agents propose modifications in parallel at heterogeneous temperatures behind a deterministic selector that gates applicability, scope, compilability, and size. Experiments across paper review, coding, math grading, robotics reward design, and financial audit-risk assessment show that MetaShare's median matches or exceeds DGM-Hyperagents'final performance and reaches its own best-performing agent in 1.7--2.9$\\times$ fewer evolution iterations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e4b0a67c17a0ec5b8f767f4b214549e41bde38fc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24007", "CorpusId": 293687160}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e4b0a67c17a0ec5b8f767f4b214549e41bde38fc", "title": "Federation over Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24007, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2321176487", "name": "Di-Xi Yao"}, {"authorId": "71520803", "name": "Tahseen Rabbani"}, {"authorId": "2276532083", "name": "Tian Li"}], "abstract": "Modern LLM-powered agents increasingly improve through experience sharing, where distributed agents distill local reasoning experience into reusable knowledge, such as a shared insight library. Existing approaches, however, still heavily rely on manually designed prompts and workflows that determine both how local agents extract useful reasoning traces and how a central/global agent aggregates them to obtain common patterns and insights. We propose Federation over Harness (FoH), a framework that automatically evolves the harness for both local and global components in this distributed multi-agent reasoning pipeline. A server agent evolves the client harness for task solving and self-reflection while, at the same time, evolving its own harness for aggregating distributed reflections, maintaining shared knowledge, and updating subsequent client harnesses. Importantly, FoH requires no extra validation signals, such as ground-truth labels or validation data, during evolution; it learns solely from self-generated reflections produced by distributed agents. Experiments on multiple benchmarks show that FoH consistently improves over single-agent baselines with a fixed harness and with harness self-evolution by 53.57% and 29.76%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b0f8cef804e1602ef873787ea11008cd57105183", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19591", "CorpusId": 292902832}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b0f8cef804e1602ef873787ea11008cd57105183", "title": "L3M: A Verifiable Harness and Runtime for Self-Extending and Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19591, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "12210412", "name": "Carson Eisenach"}, {"authorId": "2292196316", "name": "R. George"}, {"authorId": "1398008862", "name": "Vincent Quenneville-B\u00e9lair"}, {"authorId": "2459197133", "name": "Kate Gwimm"}, {"authorId": "2261495467", "name": "Dean P. Foster"}], "abstract": "The lasting gains in AI have come from methods that absorb more computation, and language models already scale computation within a call. Useful work, though, happens across calls: over hours or days, through tools and external systems, among many agents and the humans they work for, and increasingly through code the agents write themselves. Whether that work compounds is enabled by the harness around it, and the harness faces a constraint the model does not: of the three participants in such a computation, the human, the model and code, only the human's attention does not scale, so the computation can grow only if the human is not in every loop. L3M is a runtime built on that premise, written in Lean 4 so that one language implements the harness, states its invariants, and is the language agents extend it in. One computation spans humans, agents, models and code, and all of its state lives in Git: a rich state trajectory -- rather than a chat conversation -- is an object the runtime can resume, rewind, fork, compare and merge. Waiting, communication, delegation and undo are primitives, not patterns the model reasons through each time. When an agent works out a procedure, it can write it once as a sealed Lean program whose type is its permission request, and the harness offers it back as a tool -- machinery for the model to use. To take the human out of the loop, L3M uses reversibility to decide where that is safe: a tool's review tier follows from how far its effects can be undone, so agents act freely where the runtime can recover. Because the machinery can change, the properties that make this safe are stated as kernel-checked theorems about the code that runs. On Terminal-Bench, an untuned L3M reaches about two thirds of its (unsafe) shell-equipped pass rate using only safe (not needing human review) tools."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8d439df8b171997f54811f4fd3638b8edcf077d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20294", "CorpusId": 292902597}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8d439df8b171997f54811f4fd3638b8edcf077d", "title": "MMLA-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through RTT and RTMA", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20294, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2243937605", "name": "Jun-Yi Zou"}, {"authorId": "2445701977", "name": "Avrova Donz"}], "abstract": "An agent can improve its behavior within an unresolved problem by changing how it acts and which experience it retains. We propose MMLA-RSI, a recursive self-improvement scheme with two implementations in Memory-Mediated Learning Architecture: Reasoning-Time Training (RTT) changes the policy state $\\Phi$, while Reasoning-Time Memory Adaptation (RTMA) changes authoritative memory $M$. Each route also makes its adaptation method an object of improvement. A current numerical updater trains the policy proposing its successor; a current memory manager chooses the records available to the process proposing its successor. An independently admitted successor takes over the same role in the next generation. We give bounded executable rule languages, shared task/proposal interfaces, and one algorithm for either route or their composition. The RTT instance changes scalar schedules in a clipped-momentum update. The RTMA instance chooses NULL or a complete-row write with possible eviction. A fixed interpreter, trusted committer, and independent evaluator bound what can change. State versions, read closures, and conditional properties describe causal execution, accepted transitions, and finite work. Existing numerical-component studies motivate the construction. In four-candidate program selection, retaining LARC feedback updates reduces expected error by 24.65 and 36.65 percentage points for two trained initializations. In chronological continuous-integration replay, feedback binding is positive while retention increases loss. Completed SQL studies improve substantially over an unadapted substrate but do not establish the required additional online benefit. These results characterize their declared components. The two recursive routes and their joint realization are specified here; their beneficial recursive chains and discovery efficiency remain to be evaluated."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19951", "CorpusId": 292903495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "title": "AtomSearch: Evidence-Grounded Tree Search for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497901572", "name": "Jiang-Shuai-Chen-Rui-,-Zhang-Rong-,-Wang-Gui-Fang- Tong"}, {"authorId": "2430689890", "name": "Sen Deng"}, {"authorId": "2469306138", "name": "Ming-Pei Peng"}, {"authorId": "2311866886", "name": "Jian-Feng Pan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills adapt frozen models through editable external instructions. Revising several rules at once makes changes difficult to inspect, while judging each local edit only by its immediate score can discard useful intermediate states. We introduce AtomSearch, which keeps revision actions local while using subsequent skill states to guide search. Contrastive atomic editing links each primitive rule edit to failed and successful executions, a behavioral hypothesis, and an intended scope. Skill-State Tree Search (STS) evaluates short edit compositions and selectively retains them as executable states that can be revisited and extended. Outcome memory supplies later proposals with observed repair and regression statistics. We evaluate AtomSearch across four tasks and five target models. With deepseek-v4-flash, it exceeds SkillOpt on three of four tasks, including a 9.68-percentage-point gain on LiveMathematicianBench, and SkillOpt's final skills contain 2.3-19.7 times as many characters. In 67% of completed searches, the best validation state passes through an ancestor that was not highest-scoring at its depth. These results show how local rule edits can support the exploration of useful revision paths and yield compact skills, with additional offline evaluations used to explore alternative states."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18792", "CorpusId": 292895945}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "title": "SEAR: Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18792, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509898711", "name": "Xin-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2261387782", "name": "Bang-Zheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2506680028", "name": "Han-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2504090520", "name": "Lin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2196998835", "name": "Andrea Yaoyun Cui"}, {"authorId": "2510651346", "name": "Zheng-Gang Tang"}, {"authorId": "8983218", "name": "S. Buch"}, {"authorId": "2510659666", "name": "De-Jia Xu"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents can now improve robots on their own: they write control code, test it, and keep what works as skills and memories for the next task. We introduce SEAR (Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics), a framework and benchmark built around two questions. Is the agent really learning, or just trying more often? And does what it learns still help on new tasks, in a new simulator, and on a real robot? The framework runs an agent in three modes: it writes code that becomes the policy (code-as-policy), acts live in the control loop (agent-as-policy), or works as a researcher that fine-tunes a vision-language-action model (auto-research). The benchmark adds controls that separate retained experience from repeated attempts, familiar tasks, privileged simulator state, larger budgets, and luck. Across seven frontier language models, 330 simulated and five physical tasks, and over four years of agent runtime, we highlight three findings. First, the learning curve of a robotic agent depends both on the overall time budget and the task difficulty relative to model capability. Harder tasks make longer sessions worthwhile; on easier tasks, re-rolling short attempts does just as well. Second, control code and shared routines transfer across tasks and simulators, while task-specific solutions do not. On a real robot, we observe a performance gap between agent-designed practice and novel human-designed scenes. Third, different models evolve more effectively in different ways. Some do best acting live, others writing code first. And a text-only model keeps pace with a comparable multimodal one: perception helps with error assessment, but agentic reasoning and tool use can matter more. We release the benchmark and codebase to support future research."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aa3fcf09927bc0e17e69b4fcb09197209e4c8f32", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18097", "CorpusId": 292895177}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa3fcf09927bc0e17e69b4fcb09197209e4c8f32", "title": "APT: Feedback-Driven Agentic Post-Training for Text-to-Image Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18097, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2263833994", "name": "Si-Rui Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2239197291", "name": "Fang-Yun Wei"}, {"authorId": "2333464886", "name": "Dong Chen"}, {"authorId": "2256929424", "name": "Jin-Jing Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2268627199", "name": "Yi-Tong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2293940941", "name": "Yu-Nuo Chen"}, {"authorId": "2292202061", "name": "Bai-Ning Guo"}, {"authorId": "2371989220", "name": "Zheng-Jun Zha"}], "abstract": "Post-training is crucial for improving text-to-image models, yet existing pipelines typically rely on manually curated training data and fixed training distributions. We introduce APT (Agentic Post-Training), a fully automated framework that adaptively post-trains a base model according to its evolving capability profile. APT coordinates three agents to construct evaluation sets from user requirements, diagnose model-specific strengths and failure modes, and generate reinforcement learning data with an adaptive prompt distribution that emphasizes underperforming capabilities. By repeatedly evaluating, diagnosing, and updating the generator, APT lets the current model's behavior shape its next training tasks, forming a feedback loop inspired by recursive self-improvement. For general-purpose settings without user-specified evaluation scenarios, we further develop an evaluation harness that enables broad and diverse evaluation set generation. Experiments across multiple base models, benchmarks, and generation architectures demonstrate that APT is model-agnostic, benchmark-agnostic, architecture-agnostic, and fully automated."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f2b0819c5218bc3188ea778bc847b50bd1806a3a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17718", "CorpusId": 292894029}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f2b0819c5218bc3188ea778bc847b50bd1806a3a", "title": "ECG-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Targeted Electrocardiogram Waveform Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17718, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering", "Biology"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2492914543", "name": "Xiao-Liang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2509370318", "name": "Zhuo Chen"}, {"authorId": "2238892973", "name": "Chuan-Yang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2176570558", "name": "Jiang-Wei Lao"}, {"authorId": "2510690343", "name": "Quan-Bin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2325915210", "name": "Hong-Lin Lin"}, {"authorId": "2273557310", "name": "Jia-Jia Liu"}, {"authorId": "2375690800", "name": "Zhi-Hong Lu"}, {"authorId": "2352266480", "name": "Hao Jin"}, {"authorId": "2388277041", "name": "Jian Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269769748", "name": "Jin-Jie Gu"}, {"authorId": "2325195689", "name": "Li-Jun Wu"}], "abstract": "Electrocardiogram (ECG) models need supervision that targets evolving weaknesses and remains faithful to waveform evidence. We introduce ECG-RSI, a recursive self-improvement framework combining verified waveform synthesis with feedback-guided intervention search. Signal remeasurement verifies waveform edits; construction errors guide generator repair, while learner errors motivate interventions compared on a separate selection set. Across seven benchmarks, three rounds raise Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's mean question-answering accuracy from 34.57% to 56.04% and mean classification F1 from 24.27 to 34.03. Across the retained trajectory, the preferred supervision shifts from direct reinforcement to confusion-focused contrasts. Continued feedback correction yields diminishing external gains. These results motivate separating error diagnosis from intervention selection and checking whether gains on the feedback set transfer to independent benchmarks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "712d89f0018f495d506099be2d5a727b2736c545", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18845", "CorpusId": 292895030}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/712d89f0018f495d506099be2d5a727b2736c545", "title": "The Last Remote Sensing Model Built by Humans? Casting Any Earth Observation Task as Recursive Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18845, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2262009613", "name": "Si-Jie Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2295379216", "name": "Ze-Qi Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2292393163", "name": "He-Li Qi"}, {"authorId": "2186399859", "name": "Xuan-Chao Fu"}, {"authorId": "2373747309", "name": "Zhuo Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2239157968", "name": "Jun-Shi Xia"}, {"authorId": "2327165426", "name": "Jun-Jue Wang"}, {"authorId": "1387974253", "name": "Weihao Xuan"}, {"authorId": "2308101237", "name": "Naoto Yokoya"}], "abstract": "Earth observation (EO) models are still built by hand. For each new task, experts design a training recipe and tune it against a benchmark. We show that this loop can be handed to an agent. Any verifiable task can be cast as a harness and a verifier, where the harness defines what the agent may use and the verifier defines what counts as success. Every EO task can be made verifiable, through labeled benchmarks, independent sensors, physical models or the repeated observation of the same place. Given only these two components, a general-purpose coding agent builds the EO model and improves it recursively on its own outputs. Settings long treated as separate research problems, from learning with a handful of labels to adapting across domains, become choices of harness and verifier. On six EO benchmarks spanning optical, SAR and multispectral time-series imagery, with outputs that range from land-cover maps and ship instances to canopy heights and answers about large images, agent-built models surpass the state of the art on every one, and on land-cover mapping, they do so from 11% of the training labels. This establishes a new paradigm for EO, in which people design harnesses and verifiers and agents build the models, so EO models improve as agents do."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17370", "CorpusId": 292896173}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "title": "HarnessTTT: Learn to Optimize Harness at Test-Time for Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17370, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2269426296", "name": "Ying-Zi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2146176464", "name": "Yu-Long Cao"}, {"authorId": "49088449", "name": "Nanxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2359179354", "name": "Fang Wu"}, {"authorId": "48378494", "name": "Yi-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2347006409", "name": "James Y. Zou"}, {"authorId": "2382829310", "name": "Ye-Jin Choi"}, {"authorId": "2273519476", "name": "Chao-Wei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Scientific discovery with language models requires finding the best valid solution within a finite computational budget. We study how feedback from this search can improve the procedure used to conduct it. We introduce HarnessTTT, a test-time learning framework that trains a harness proposer while keeping the executor frozen. The proposer generates executable harnesses comprising system prompts, reusable skills, tools, middleware, and execution controls. Each harness guides a budgeted discovery rollout, whose outcome provides feedback for updating the proposer. A discovery-oriented policy-gradient objective combines gap-normalized rewards, leave-one-out advantages (RLOO), and max-sharpening to favor high-performing proposals. Generated components are validated before execution, and the search retains the best valid solution and selected harnesses across rounds. Across fourteen discovery tasks in mathematics, systems, GPU kernels, heuristic programming, astrodynamics, and biology, HarnessTTT with a frozen 9B executor improves on OpenEvolve with the same executor on all eleven benchmark tasks (e.g., from 1.17 to 2.64 on circle packing), improves on the discovery-fine-tuned Finch-9B by up to 92%, holds the best open-weight sub-10B result on nine, and reaches 0.380919 on Erd\\H{o}s minimum overlap, better than the best human reference of 0.380927. Scaling the proposer and frozen executor to 27B improves on eleven of fourteen tasks, and transferring the learned harnesses to GPT-5.5 matches or exceeds the strongest reported system of any backbone on nine of fourteen tasks. Together, these results show that harness search can itself be learned at test time: a trained proposer lets a small frozen executor search effectively, and the harnesses it finds carry over to stronger executors."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "037ec1df7fba251ee5a0733c3d286565c30a4a06", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16725", "CorpusId": 292895595}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/037ec1df7fba251ee5a0733c3d286565c30a4a06", "title": "Trace-to-Architecture: Capability Bottleneck Diagnosis for Harness Revision", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16725, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510648559", "name": "Wen-Feng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510688186", "name": "Jian-Rui Shi"}, {"authorId": "2449506805", "name": "Min-Gang Guo"}, {"authorId": "2334848346", "name": "Jiayuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2483089487", "name": "Yu-Qi Lu"}, {"authorId": "2304512232", "name": "Jia-Jun Sun"}, {"authorId": "2510649554", "name": "Guo-Jiang Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2510493639", "name": "Jing Li"}], "abstract": "Improving an LLM agent requires knowing what to change when it fails. Similar failures can require different fixes, and resolving one problem can expose another. We introduce Trace-to-Architecture (T2A), a framework that uses execution trajectories to determine what to change in an agent's harness and when those changes apply. A development agent diagnoses failures using execution evidence from successful and failed tasks, revises the corresponding harness components, and then checks whether target tasks improve, selected successful tasks remain successful, and the edits are actually triggered. New execution evidence guides subsequent diagnosis and revision. Across ALFWorld, ScienceWorld, and AppWorld, T2A achieves the highest aggregate score for all three evaluated task agents, exceeding their respective strongest baselines by 6.2--30.3 points. We also integrate T2A's diagnosis and validation procedures into Meta-Harness, improving the resulting harnesses'mean ScienceWorld score by 21.6 points from the same starting harness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15964", "CorpusId": 292895466}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "title": "Small but Mighty: Test Suites as Improvement Signals for Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15964, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2249879747", "name": "Wei Cheng"}, {"authorId": "3007026", "name": "Wen-Chao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2313576252", "name": "Da-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2249915042", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}], "abstract": "A self-evolving agent rewrites its own harness and must decide which rewrites to keep, so its improvement signal is the check a rewrite has to pass. Verifying a rewrite by agent rollout is expensive and subjective, while executable checks such as the repository's own tests are too sparse to catch behavior-changing rewrites, rejecting only a quarter of them. We instead take test feedback as the executable signal, and search for test suites that are both small and strong with a quality-diversity evolutionary algorithm, so that the signal is dense and informative. TeSearch treats suite size as a behavior descriptor, so that quality is pursued at every size and a small, strong suite is reachable, and learns a transition kernel during the search that denoises the LLM as an actuator on that descriptor. Across 129 real-world modules and three backbones, TeSearch delivers the highest-quality suites of any method compared, LLM-driven evolutionary search included, and the smallest of any LLM-based one. Used as the improvement signal of a self-evolving agent, it rejects 59.9% of behavior-changing rewrites against 25.3% for the repository's tests, at a 4.0% false-alarm rate and 3.5 s per decision. The code and data are available at https://github.com/ccsnow127/TeSearch."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15708", "CorpusId": 292894063}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "title": "SpecForge: Recursive Self-Improvement for Coding Agents via Behavioral Specifications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15708, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2204622281", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2304180290", "name": "Peng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2283249309", "name": "Wei Cheng"}], "abstract": "Coding agents based on frozen language models can improve their performance by refining the external context they rely on, without updating model weights. We study recursive self-improvement through behavioral specifications: natural-language code documentation that describes the behavior of program entities and guides agents in generating correct code. We formulate a self-improvement loop in which an agent generates code from its current specifications, evaluates it with execution-verified tests, and refines the specifications based on observed failures. The improved specifications then guide subsequent generation, progressively improving the agent's understanding of the codebase. However, recursive refinement is challenging because program entities are interdependent. Improving the specification of one entity can inadvertently degrade the correctness of others, a phenomenon we call output coupling. This creates a whack-a-mole effect in which local improvements cause regressions elsewhere, preventing consistent progress. We introduce SpecForge, a multi-frontier tree search framework that addresses this challenge with three mechanisms: (i) Pareto-guided state selection preserves complementary specification states and prevents premature commitment; (ii) dependency-constrained entity selection prioritizes callee-before-caller refinement; and (iii) error-diversified expansion explores multiple refinements using different subsets of test failures. On DevEval+, SpecForge achieves the best performance across five backbone models, including a 94.9% solve rate with Claude-4.5-Sonnet and a 5.7-percentage-point average improvement over the strongest baseline. The improved specifications also make the agent better at tasks outside the loop: they transfer to cross-language translation and to new-feature implementation, where they outperform the benchmark-provided requirement documents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e13e0fbcf9575bab83e4f529e5a581cd2eb05f04", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14662", "CorpusId": 292858880}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e13e0fbcf9575bab83e4f529e5a581cd2eb05f04", "title": "Does Failure Feedback Direct Agent Self-Modification?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14662, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2502175281", "name": "Rong He"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agents use failure feedback to revise their code or instructions, but gains after selection do not establish target-specific repair or generalization. We measure directedness by comparing repair of a randomly selected target failure with repair of the parent's other failures. Across 145 eligible modifications in public Darwin G\\\"odel Machine logs, we find no detectable target-specific advantage. Controlled instruction-revision experiments with small open models show that relevant feedback can improve target repair. In a second study with a pre-specified analysis plan, target feedback raised repair by 20 percentage points for Qwen3-4B relative to matched same-category feedback; the 7-point increase for Phi-4-mini was not significant. Target repair did not reliably imply held-out improvement. Held-out gains were concentrated in settings with identifiable, repairable failure mechanisms, including planted errors and natural tool-interface failures. Benefits of edit localization and regression screening varied across settings, while eight-step revision loops and GEPA ended at similar mean held-out accuracy. Evaluations should report target repair, transfer to related failures, and held-out net change separately."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ba860412dba54b4dac209c0758990b8240360522", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14547", "CorpusId": 292859443}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba860412dba54b4dac209c0758990b8240360522", "title": "A Self-Defining Operator for Distributed Applications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14547, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2321931754", "name": "Shi-Hang Li"}, {"authorId": "2496283641", "name": "Thomas Anderson"}, {"authorId": "2257031542", "name": "Ratul Mahajan"}, {"authorId": "2287307058", "name": "Simon Peter"}, {"authorId": "2347353506", "name": "Luke S. Zettlemoyer"}], "abstract": "To operate distributed applications with high availability, operators must rapidly diagnose and mitigate any incident. LLM-based agents can help, but unlike experienced human operators they do not improve as they handle more incidents. Each investigation starts from scratch, re-deriving likely causes and re-testing hypotheses even when a similar fault was resolved in the past. We present a self-defining operator (SDO) that improves with time by updating its operational behavior and memory. When SDO resolves an incident, it distills that experience into playbooks, which describe reusable diagnostic instructions for future incidents with similar faults, and detectors, scripts that take application telemetry as input and output relevant playbook(s). SDO continuously runs the detectors and invokes LLMs only when one fires, so its token cost scales with incident count rather than with operational time. Across 11 open-source microservice applications, SDO cuts resolution time by 52% on recurring faults while keeping per-incident cost low. SDO is open source at https://github.com/self-defining-systems/sdo."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13361", "CorpusId": 292830518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "title": "AgentSWE: Can Coding Agents Build the Agent You Actually Want?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13361, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2445720410", "name": "Jia-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374412422", "name": "Hong-Jin Qian"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2321653170", "name": "Jia-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362012611", "name": "Zheng Liu"}], "abstract": "As the production of software passes into the hands of software itself, coding agents are increasingly commissioned to build agents fitted to their users'own workflows. This delegation carries a quiet assumption, that the delivered agent will faithfully serve the requirement it was built for, rather than merely appear to. No existing benchmark tests that assumption. We introduce AgentSWE, a benchmark for agent software engineering spanning three stages of the agent development lifecycle: Creation of a complete agent from a natural-language requirement, Editing of production agent codebases, and Optimization toward a measurable target. Each of its 25 tasks is framed as a commission rather than an exam: the builder iterates against a handful of illustrative cases, while acceptance exercises the frozen submission on held-out cases designed to span the full requirement, grading behavior against requirement-derived rubrics under hard pass/fail gates or, for Optimization, against the source benchmark's own objective. Evaluating a range of coding agents across models and harnesses, we find that none comes close to delivering what a requirement asks for: the gap between what a builder demonstrates and what it delivers is the norm, not the corner case. An accompanying empirical analysis of the failure trajectories identifies recurring failure modes, among them overfitting to the visible cases, hollow runtimes behind code that looks complete, and unsupported claims of completion, each a signal by which coding agents can learn to build the agents they are asked for."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14224", "CorpusId": 292859473}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "title": "Harness^2: Recursive Agent Harnessing for an Open World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14224, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2319451284", "name": "Rui-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2258603407", "name": "Yan-Fei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2220095956", "name": "Zhongying CuiZhu"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2321601631", "name": "Yifei Ming"}, {"authorId": "2453417135", "name": "Han Yu"}, {"authorId": "2325953181", "name": "Ru-Jun Han"}, {"authorId": "2278969944", "name": "Chen-Yu Lee"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}], "abstract": "LLM agents'capabilities depend not only on their underlying models but also on their execution harnesses. Recursive self-improvement (RSI) offers a promising approach to automatically evolve these harnesses. However, deploying RSI in open-world settings still faces two critical challenges. First, open-world tasks are typically unseen during the harness design phase and novel at test time, leaving no ground-truth verifiers to reliably guide online harness edits. Second, the RSI process remains highly task-specific and fails to generalize to novel scenarios. We introduce Harness^2, a plug-and-play, recursive agent harnessing framework that addresses these challenges through two connected levels of recursion. Task-level recursion refines the current task's harness using a contrastive Propose-Probe-Compose step that reliably guides edits without ground-truth verifiers, and can be scaled further via parallel or sequential recursion. Harnessing-level recursion then extracts experience from these edits to update a continual improvement harness, enabling the agent to better adapt to future tasks. Across three professional benchmarks and four model-harness configurations, a single step consistently improves GLM-5 and Gemini 3.5 Flash over their base harnesses, yielding gains of up to 11.5 points on WorkBuddy-Bench, 4.5 points on JobBench, and 11.8 percentage points on LAB, with further gains as the task-level recursion budget increases."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "192fd3b5d9dbad85b386fdda9c244e0ad67f3e3d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12341", "CorpusId": 292737748}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/192fd3b5d9dbad85b386fdda9c244e0ad67f3e3d", "title": "Can AI Agents Learn Their Way to the Top? Evaluating Heuristic Learning in a Long-Running Game Agent Competition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12341, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2383097217", "name": "Kai-Sen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2445721255", "name": "Qing-Le Liu"}, {"authorId": "2502195351", "name": "Ke-Jin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2375397262", "name": "Yi-Chen Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2509331205", "name": "Jie-Ming Li"}, {"authorId": "2412815574", "name": "Sheng-Han Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2509362532", "name": "Rui-Ze Yang"}, {"authorId": "2494136801", "name": "Bo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2509317357", "name": "Heng Gong"}, {"authorId": "2506648206", "name": "Xiang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2509365018", "name": "Lan-Yue Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2371114650", "name": "Kai-Yu Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2504006281", "name": "Zhuo-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2509358622", "name": "Shao-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2509349517", "name": "Cheng-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2320707357", "name": "Yong Yan"}, {"authorId": "2509366300", "name": "Wei-Xuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2313639472", "name": "Tian-Yuan Luo"}, {"authorId": "2499252664", "name": "Si-Meng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2537322", "name": "You-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2404231718", "name": "Si-Han Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2455430554", "name": "Sheng-Yuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371219369", "name": "Huan-Ang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2214082934", "name": "Jiazheng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2509345208", "name": "Xiao-Hui Xie"}, {"authorId": "2509387122", "name": "Wen-Tao Han"}, {"authorId": "2253869803", "name": "Hong-Ning Wang"}], "abstract": "Adversarial games have driven advances from heuristic search to reinforcement learning, yet learning and adapting strategies from limited samples remain challenging. AI agents offer an alternative by turning game experience into revisions of executable policies. Building on heuristic learning (HL), we formalize Adversarial Heuristic Learning (AHL), a paradigm that uses AI agents as learning engines to refine game policies and supporting software while keeping model weights fixed. We introduce AAArena, a benchmark comprising 12 authentic adversarial games and 1,920 archived human programs, with an evaluation protocol modeled on real-world game competitions. Agents interpret rules, choose opponents, analyze replays, and revise game agents to achieve their highest ranking within fixed match and evaluation budgets. We evaluate \\val{completedmodels} model and harness configurations: Opus5.5 with Claude Code earns 6 gold medals, while no evaluated configuration tops the remaining 6 human ladders. Performance is generally weaker in games with more complex rule specifications. Further experiments show that opponent selection and dense feedback support policy improvement, and that agents learn from both on-policy replays of their own matches and off-policy replays of other players'matches. These results highlight HL's potential in adversarial games and identify persistent challenges in game understanding, strategy implementation, and long-horizon policy development."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2c17c1cab5a7eac886df799614d0dd5a978ae65d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12233", "CorpusId": 292738322}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2c17c1cab5a7eac886df799614d0dd5a978ae65d", "title": "ReSI: Recursive Safety Improvement toward Resistant and Resilient AI", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12233, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2302794774", "name": "Jing-Nan Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2213708589", "name": "Dong-Cheng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367851765", "name": "Yi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2361677209", "name": "Ming Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2404001607", "name": "Qiao-Sheng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2329730696", "name": "You-Bang Sun"}, {"authorId": "2153659066", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2240825631", "name": "Xiang-Nan He"}, {"authorId": "2257036129", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}, {"authorId": "2409963947", "name": "Xia Hu"}, {"authorId": "2310664569", "name": "Bo-Wen Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2379724642", "name": "Chao-Chao Lu"}, {"authorId": "2379674258", "name": "Xiang Wang"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement, the participation of AI systems in improving their own capabilities, is beginning to move from theoretical prospect to practice, posing both challenges and opportunities for safety alignment. Models evolve through frequent updates, and their safety alignment requires continual adaptation to each new checkpoint. Meanwhile, with evolving red-teaming methods exposing new vulnerabilities, safety improvement for each checkpoint needs to mitigate exposed vulnerabilities and generalize to risks not yet revealed. Following R$^2$AI, we term these goals resistance to known threats and resilience to unforeseen risks. Recursive self-improvement, in turn, inspires an approach to both goals: safety alignment could likewise advance through successive rounds of evaluation and update. We therefore introduce ReSI, a recursive safety improvement framework that implements this approach through automated research. In each round, ReSI applies diverse red-teaming methods to identify vulnerabilities in the current target model, develops training recipes, and promotes the update with the largest safety gain among those passing a Pareto gate on capability retention as the next target model. Across four dense and mixture-of-experts models, ReSI matches or exceeds evaluated frontier models on in-distribution and out-of-distribution safety benchmarks, and outperforms alignment baselines on nearly all safety evaluations while largely preserving general capabilities. In particular, ReSI reduces the mean X-Teaming attack success rate across the four models from 86.01% to 31.45%, well below GPT-5.6-Luna's leading frontier result of 56.69%, indicating stronger resilience to attacks unseen during training. These findings support recursive safety improvement as a practical path toward resistant and resilient AI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0f843fe4a7dd9ecd173624a03f5f4cf992321b5b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09426", "CorpusId": 292694017}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0f843fe4a7dd9ecd173624a03f5f4cf992321b5b", "title": "RSI-Forge: From Research Papers to Environments for Recursive Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09426, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508875462", "name": "Ren-Xiong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2323375728", "name": "Darvin Yi"}, {"authorId": "2508407905", "name": "Abril Herrlein"}, {"authorId": "2253468963", "name": "Anas Mahmoud"}, {"authorId": "2214053906", "name": "Advait Gosai"}, {"authorId": "2508409382", "name": "Lisiman Hua"}, {"authorId": "2261083322", "name": "MohammadHossein Rezaei"}, {"authorId": "2116513714", "name": "Xing-Ming Guo"}, {"authorId": "2309003931", "name": "Anisha Gunjal"}, {"authorId": "2385566544", "name": "Utkarsh Tyagi"}, {"authorId": "2410257847", "name": "David J. Lee"}, {"authorId": "2345288933", "name": "Minglai Yang"}, {"authorId": "2290076061", "name": "Haris Riaz"}, {"authorId": "2499630683", "name": "Chen-Guang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2333859426", "name": "Hua-Xiu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2260757766", "name": "D. Zhang"}, {"authorId": "150029223", "name": "Aakash Sabharwal"}, {"authorId": "2395868627", "name": "Tong Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2382827260", "name": "Yun-Zhong He"}], "abstract": "Environments are the foundation of recursive self-improvement: they provide the problems agents work on and the feedback used to evaluate progress. Yet constructing challenging research environments with reliable evaluation still depends on domain experts, limiting their scale and disciplinary coverage. We introduce RSI-Forge, a multi-agent pipeline that turns published papers into executable environments for self-improvement. Three agents coordinate construction, reproduction, and review to produce tasks with automated evaluators; each paper's method is independently reimplemented to establish a baseline score. We present 210 environments across 18 fields, including 90 reviewed by independent human domain experts. Both experts and agent judges give high ratings to the potential for improving the provided starting solutions and the evaluators'ability to distinguish solution quality, whereas experts are more critical of shortcut resistance, faithfulness to the source paper, and whether a single idea can exhaust a task. To validate their use for repeated improvement, we evaluate four models over 3 successive attempts on 120 environments, with each attempt inheriting prior code and notes while model weights remain fixed. At least one model improves after the first attempt in 84% of environments. Models also outperform the reproduced paper methods in 68 of the 120 environments, demonstrating room for gains beyond these baselines. Transcript analysis identifies work beyond parameter tuning in 95% of these successful attempts. Analysis of the resulting trajectories shows that models scoring lower on these tasks explore less, more often accept gains smaller than the reported standard error, and rely more heavily on tuning to the development set. RSI-Forge provides a scalable approach to constructing research environments for training and evaluating self-improving agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "32a034433c0a6b782f08554e0c7c8855686581ca", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09239", "CorpusId": 292695308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/32a034433c0a6b782f08554e0c7c8855686581ca", "title": "The Winner's Curse in LLM Self-Improvement Loops: Selection Noise, Lock-in, and Acceptance Rules", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09239, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508874789", "name": "Li-Tao Hu"}, {"authorId": "2119311140", "name": "Yu-Tong Tang"}], "abstract": "Self-improving LLM systems propose changes to themselves and keep those that score better on a small evaluation set. We treat this keep-if-better step as selection under measurement noise, model the correlated errors of the candidates in a single decision, and study empirically what happens when the evaluation set is reused. In runs where Qwen models rewrite their own instructions and every candidate is also scored on 600 held-out items, most proposals after the first are harmful, and the model gives the size of the winner's curse of a generation's best candidate. With a prior from a separate pilot, it matches the average overstatement of first-generation commits in native loops, though not setting by setting. In a pre-registered study, the final selection-set score of greedy loops exceeded held-out accuracy by 13 to 20 points with 16 selection items and by 1 to 5 points with 256. Held-out gains grew with the selection set on TREC but not on GSM8K, and the tested acceptance rules did not beat greedy acceptance over whole runs. Gains measured on the selection set also exceeded held-out gains when a current model refined a competent instruction, and in the validation scores of GEPA and MIPROv2. Scoring the starting and the current instruction on 64 items never used for selection removes the average bias of a loop's reported gain, but single estimates remain off by about 6 points. Self-improvement studies should report held-out gains with their uncertainty."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "821e76bc311a1bd3b525f8aba91e990751307b72", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.10611", "CorpusId": 292732733}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/821e76bc311a1bd3b525f8aba91e990751307b72", "title": "Verification and Self-Improvement in Agentic AI: Foundations and Limits", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10611, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2218344381", "name": "Chien-Ping Lu"}], "abstract": "A self-improving agent can find accepted solutions more often without expanding the tasks its verification interface can accept. We extend task-relative verification with explicit support and transcript interfaces to distinguish search gains from changes in accepted-task coverage. A stage's native reach contains tasks accepted with default support; its closure frontier contains tasks accepted with any support the declared interface permits. A semantic containment criterion identifies the authority or exposure condition that every verified escape must violate, while guard-transfer invariance prevents a change of representation from being counted as a capability expansion. An executable Boolean-program repair agent makes the distinction concrete: over 36,864 paired runs, counterexample feedback reduces verifier calls without changing coverage, whereas an enforced permission change admits a new target with a proof excluding every old-interface program. Exact and uniformly gapped randomized verification share this account; independent majority amplification improves reliability while preserving the accepted language. Separate adaptive-selection contracts state what is additionally required to interpret a proposed successor's score as evidence of deployment improvement. Classical hierarchy results and finite audits provide supporting boundaries. The framework specifies the evidence needed to establish improved search, wider accepted capability, and reliable successor selection."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e27ae00aade4a6ed0c94b4a8c0ed169ae2006603", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09197", "CorpusId": 292694730}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e27ae00aade4a6ed0c94b4a8c0ed169ae2006603", "title": "FreeEvolve: Learning to Evolve Beyond Fixed Loops", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09197, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2164063663", "name": "Lecheng Kong"}, {"authorId": "2456704427", "name": "Li-Ke Hui"}, {"authorId": "2273556743", "name": "Nikos Kanakaris"}, {"authorId": "47487418", "name": "Prithwish Jana"}, {"authorId": "2325953477", "name": "Sahika Genc"}, {"authorId": "2074296975", "name": "Narayanan Sadagopan"}], "abstract": "Agent evolvers automate the design of the prompts, skills and workflows around language model agents, yet the optimization process they follow is still designed by hand: a fixed search loop decides how candidates are evaluated, which are kept and when the search stops. We propose FREEEVOLVE, which automates this process as well. An environment specifies the goal, target agent, evaluator, data and resource limits; within these limits, the evolver itself decides what to test, how much evidence to collect, which candidates to pursue and when to stop. These decisions follow an editable evolution skill, which we improve through meta-evolution by scoring each candidate skill on the fresh target agent it produces. The optimization process thus becomes a capability learned from experience rather than a loop engineered in advance. On tau3-bench, ARC-AGI-2, ARC-AGI-3 and Terminal-Bench 2.1, FREEEVOLVE controls the evolution campaign by itself, yet improves the primary held-out metric by 13.6 points on average and matches or exceeds hand-designed evolvers. The learned process keeps improving with experience: meta-evolved skills add 6.9 points over the seed skill on fresh target agents, demonstrating transferability across environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "990cb86a6f20a9e88e685cdfe45ac734fd85c68b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.08902", "CorpusId": 292694189}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/990cb86a6f20a9e88e685cdfe45ac734fd85c68b", "title": "Agent Plasticity: Measuring Self-Improvement Through Experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08902, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2311481779", "name": "Harman Singh"}, {"authorId": "152918929", "name": "A. Bakhtin"}, {"authorId": "2375136486", "name": "Ru-Lin Shao"}, {"authorId": "2282478", "name": "Gabriel Synnaeve"}, {"authorId": "2308102420", "name": "Ilia Kulikov"}, {"authorId": "2273646684", "name": "Rob Fergus"}, {"authorId": "2261737783", "name": "Sanjeev Arora"}, {"authorId": "2242659602", "name": "Kurt Keutzer"}, {"authorId": "2243265350", "name": "Jason E. Weston"}, {"authorId": "2827015", "name": "Anuj Mahajan"}, {"authorId": "2315131630", "name": "Anirudh Goyal"}], "abstract": "AI agents increasingly operate in environments where they can diagnose failures and improve through experience, yet existing evaluations largely measure what an agent can do at a fixed point in time rather than how effectively it learns. Evaluating self-improvement requires answering three questions: does future performance improve and generalize beyond the interactions that enabled learning; how efficiently are new capabilities acquired; and where does the self-improvement process break down? To answer these questions, we study self-improvement in a controlled setting where agents amortize past experience into reusable artifacts that are inherited by future instances. At each checkpoint, we measure performance on training and held-out environment interactions while accounting for learning cost. We introduce agent plasticity, the efficiency with which an agent converts experience into gains in future held-out performance. Across multiple environments, frontier models exhibit sharply different improvement trajectories despite comparable opportunities to learn. Some achieve substantial and persistent gains, while others remain near or below their initial performance, and gains within the training regime often transfer only partially to out-of-distribution conditions. Endpoint capability and acquisition efficiency also diverge: the agent that ultimately performs best need not be the one that improves most efficiently. Tracing failures through the improvement loop further reveals different candidate bottlenecks. Agents with low plasticity often fail to reuse relevant artifacts, whereas more plastic agents may still fail despite reusing relevant artifacts, pointing to limitations in artifact quality, generalization, or application. Evaluating self-improving agents requires measuring not only what they can do, but how effectively they become better through experience."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "de5c374a68304dfcca71c6395a58df15f77abc15", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.08995", "CorpusId": 292694681}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/de5c374a68304dfcca71c6395a58df15f77abc15", "title": "PhysEvo: Astra Can Act, Let It", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08995, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2363136586", "name": "Wen-Qing Tian"}, {"authorId": "2509131038", "name": "Ze-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2118393734", "name": "Zhaocheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2482143965", "name": "Fengwei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2405949467", "name": "Qiang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2258184369", "name": "Liang Wang"}], "abstract": "Astra can act, yet reliable manipulation depends on the system through which it observes and controls the world. We introduce PhysEvo, a framework for physical recursive self-improvement (RSI) around a single frozen model. A task agent executes robot tasks; a meta-agent uses the resulting trajectories to diagnose failures, revise tools and skills, and test corrections. The meta-agent can also improve its own diagnostic tools, so retained revisions support both later action and later self-improvement. This process develops joint-level control, evidence-seeking observation, and reusable manipulation skills without model-weight updates or a separately trained action policy. Across 42 RoboDojo tasks, held-out-layout evaluation of retained task-specific deployment versions yields a five-dimension average score of 68.14/100 and 62.00% success, compared with 47.17% for RoboDawn's one-shot Astra agent, the strongest published reference in our comparison. On eight manipulation tasks challenging direct Astra, PhysEvo achieves 55.00% success, compared with 1.25% for the direct-Astra reference. Deploying the simulation-evolved harness on AgileX PiPER and continuing skill revision yields 90.60/100 average score and 84.00% success across 25 trials on five real-world tasks. PhysEvo turns the consequences of action into persistent, testable changes to how a frozen model acts and improves."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "32e544e9bd6160db13f9eef67158b4615dbc995c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05039", "CorpusId": 292655502}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/32e544e9bd6160db13f9eef67158b4615dbc995c", "title": "Causal Improvement Graph for Agentic Harness Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05039, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350521366", "name": "Jun-Jie Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2128786021", "name": "Shunyu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2363879148", "name": "Hao-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2303419056", "name": "Ting-En Lin"}, {"authorId": "2237932828", "name": "Yong-Bin Li"}, {"authorId": "2354552882", "name": "Da-Cheng Tao"}], "abstract": "Agentic Harness is the runtime that constructs task context and controls execution flow, thereby shaping overall agent performance. Given a fixed model and external evaluation, automated Harness optimization seeks to improve this runtime through an iterative proposal--evaluation loop to better solve target tasks. Existing meta-harness methods mainly adopt proposer-centric discovery, in which an LLM-based proposer integrates accumulated experimental findings to determine subsequent Harness revisions. This places the burden of maintaining the evolving improvement state on the proposer as history expands and its underlying experimental logic becomes harder to discern. In this paper, we introduce the Causal Improvement Graph (CIG), a graph-governed meta-harness framework that externalizes the evolving improvement state in a persistent graph, allowing prior findings to directly govern subsequent Harness optimization through local proposer operations. CIG grows and links Evidence, Hypothesis, Intervention, and Outcome nodes to represent what was observed, how it may be explained, how to test that explanation, and what the evaluation reveals. Their structural relations preserve how the improvement state changes across iterations, allowing local proposers to build directly on relations among prior findings rather than recover them from raw history. Across various agent tasks, CIG discovers stronger Harnesses than previous meta-harness baselines and remains robust to the choice of task solver and proposer. Structural ablations further support the design of an explicit improvement state with graph-governed evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1a716edf85728ff75eefb6b40f04af721d2868db", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.04646", "CorpusId": 292654693}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a716edf85728ff75eefb6b40f04af721d2868db", "title": "SEIS: Self-Evolving Inference Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04646, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2507872054", "name": "Zhen Xu"}, {"authorId": "2301500865", "name": "Jingyu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2267686036", "name": "Zong-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "71520803", "name": "Tahseen Rabbani"}, {"authorId": "2343889050", "name": "Ce Zhang"}], "abstract": "Inference systems determine how fast and how cheaply language models can be served, so making them faster has direct practical value. However, prior work focuses mostly on optimizing certain parts such as kernels or memory within the large system. In this work, we take a holistic approach and apply agentic self-evolution to optimize the whole system end-to-end. Our SEIS (Self-Evolving Inference Systems) autonomously optimizes the entire mini-sglang engine without human intervention through iterative sessions with inherited experiences and code changes. Serving Qwen3-0.6B on H100, the resulting engine reaches 3.27X the throughput of the original mini-sglang implementation and beats SOTA engines like vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, and SGLang in the single-request workload. The correctness of the optimized inference engine by SEIS is tested in terms of numerical difference and downstream accuracy on math and long-context retrieval tasks. The code and session histories show that the speedup comes from redesigning the whole engine and that building on earlier sessions beats independent attempts. These results suggest that agentic self-evolution can optimize a complex system end-to-end. The evaluation also has to evolve with the engine, and letting agents evolve it is a natural next step."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "01bd29575dbd5ef6a95f2444b0d51082ab7c3f45", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02616", "CorpusId": 292587767}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/01bd29575dbd5ef6a95f2444b0d51082ab7c3f45", "title": "VERSE: Verified Self-Evolving Optimizer for Agent Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02616, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283130879", "name": "Ze-Kai Wang"}, {"authorId": "152988336", "name": "Ying-Qiang Ge"}, {"authorId": "2387845950", "name": "Ze-Kun Wang"}, {"authorId": "2327933756", "name": "Hai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2353317620", "name": "Yu-Hui Xu"}, {"authorId": "2507056160", "name": "Joshua Frandsen"}, {"authorId": "2507065196", "name": "Shan-Cong Fu"}, {"authorId": "2325366308", "name": "Ashia C. Wilson"}, {"authorId": "2322749934", "name": "Chandan K. Reddy"}], "abstract": "Harness evolution improves an LLM agent's prompts, tools, and workflow, while the optimizer's own tools and procedures often remain fixed. We study whether an optimizer can improve another agent more effectively by also improving how it diagnoses failures, develops edits, and tests their effects. Two observations guide our design. In a controlled study, optimizer self-evolution fails to improve performance without execution-based verification, but achieves the best result of that study when verification is available. Across five executors, self-evolving optimizers build their own tools for failure analysis, verification, training audits, and workflow control. Motivated by these findings, we introduce VERSE, a Verified Self-Evolving optimizer for agent harnesses. VERSE lets the optimizer test draft edits, replay failures, and perturb suspected steps before submission, while tracking fixes and regressions across rounds. Using this feedback, the optimizer revises both the executor harness and its own prompts, skills, tools, hooks, and notes, while the weights of the optimizer and executor models stay fixed. Under a shared protocol with disjoint training, validation, and test tasks, VERSE improves all four evaluated harness optimizers on held-out SWE-rebench tasks and newer out-of-distribution tasks in five languages. Its best validation-selected harness reaches 42.3% and 37.7% accuracy, respectively, against 39.2% and 29.3% for the strongest baselines. Code is available at https://github.com/wzekai/VERSE."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2bf87e21b4bd17f60d8ac89332c322feeef1ff70", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.03123", "CorpusId": 292590448}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2bf87e21b4bd17f60d8ac89332c322feeef1ff70", "title": "Foresight: planning future perception in streaming VLMs without retraining", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03123, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455711135", "name": "Ashok Prasad Neupane"}, {"authorId": "2507129477", "name": "Dipan Bartaula"}, {"authorId": "2507129743", "name": "Ankit Belbase"}, {"authorId": "41071419", "name": "Saugat Adhikari"}, {"authorId": "2195276543", "name": "Samip Ghimire"}, {"authorId": "2507129444", "name": "Saroj Poudel"}, {"authorId": "2078892", "name": "Binod Bhattarai"}, {"authorId": "35268081", "name": "D. Paudel"}], "abstract": "Existing streaming vision-language models (VLMs) continuously perceive and reason over visual streams, but their computational pathways remain fixed throughout inference. Consequently, they cannot adapt computation to evolving scene dynamics, where different future events demand different levels and forms of perception. We show that streaming VLMs inherently possess the ability to anticipate the immediate future, and leverage this capability to dynamically configure future computation in a training-free manner. Realizing such anticipatory computation, however, is very challenging: future anticipation must be sufficiently reliable to guide computation, planning must run concurrently with streaming inference, and online reconfiguration must incur negligible overhead. To address these challenges, we introduce FORESIGHT, a dual-stream architecture comprising two Siamese LLMs with shared weights, input encoders, and KV cache. The first LLM continuously processes incoming tokens, while the second runs ahead of the stream to anticipate future context, plan future computation, and generate task responses without interrupting streaming inference. Each plan decides when to reason next, what to check then, and how densely to sample, keeping transient evidence separate from persistent control. The resulting computation plan is executed online through an efficient reconfiguration protocol with schemaguided decoding and lightweight diff-based updates, enabling dynamic adaptation with low overhead. With a frozen Qwen3-VL-8B backbone, FORESIGHT achieves 23.0 mean joint F1 on OmniPro Online evaluation beating strongest trained baseline by 9.5%, while improving the backbone by 6.7 on StreamingBench and 15.4 on OVO-Bench, with the largest gain of 18.7 when evidence arrives later in the video stream. Our source code will be made publicly available."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ed18460ff833bbcecb34274a2b9a12488ff25355", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.03548", "CorpusId": 292590372}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ed18460ff833bbcecb34274a2b9a12488ff25355", "title": "Recursive Harness Self-Improvement for Frontier Reasoning Data Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03548, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328428018", "name": "Wen-Long Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382925293", "name": "Zheng-Bo Jiao"}, {"authorId": "2507754390", "name": "Chen-Xu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2148649304", "name": "Lekang Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2505395119", "name": "Si-Yuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2439330712", "name": "Qi-Tuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2348972082", "name": "Guo Chen"}, {"authorId": "2362426022", "name": "Lin-Feng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Generating progressively harder reasoning problems requires synthesis procedures that adapt as the task distribution evolves. Existing task-level recursion reuses generated problems as seeds but leaves the construction harness unchanged. We present task-harness co-evolution, a framework for recursive harness self-improvement (RSI) in reasoning-data synthesis. Online self-improvement converts intermediate solver failures into reusable skills during generation. Post-task self-improvement revises skills, prompts, and workflows after each batch, adopting candidates only when they generate harder valid tasks within a bounded cost increase. Model weights and verification criteria remain fixed. Across mathematics, coding, and science, mean solver accuracy decreases from 100.0% to 54.8% over fourteen evolution rounds. Ablations show that combining both update schedules produces harder tasks than fixed-harness recursion or either schedule alone. The resulting data improves downstream SFT and GRPO performance. In particular, a 27B student fine-tuned on 10K synthesized mathematics examples achieves 62.5% mean-16 accuracy on APEX, competitive with selected frontier-model references. These results support adapting the synthesis harness alongside the tasks to generate increasingly challenging data with downstream training value."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.01073", "CorpusId": 292521530}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "title": "Safety Must Survive Self-Improvement: Why Failures Persist and How Agents Recover", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01073, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2221081346", "name": "Yun-Bei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2221231969", "name": "Janet Wang"}, {"authorId": "2214534099", "name": "Saiyue Lyu"}, {"authorId": "152988336", "name": "Ying-Qiang Ge"}, {"authorId": "2087743517", "name": "Kaiqu Liang"}, {"authorId": "2278326150", "name": "Zi-Jian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2322749934", "name": "Chandan K. Reddy"}, {"authorId": "2282741360", "name": "J. Hamm"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) allows agents to carry useful changes across generations. Maintaining safety across these generations involves both preventing unsafe behavior from persisting and enabling recovery when failures occur. We study these challenges through a controlled testbed of stateful authorization tasks, where fixed LLM editors optimize executable agent components and independent traces record their effects. Paired interventions separate which revisions pass validation, which program continues running, and which program the editor revises next. After a new authorization dependency invalidates previously tested optimizations, historical scores preserve the same unsafe programs in 22 of 48 framework histories despite a correct alternative in every affected archive. Refreshing scores restores correctness on the original suite, with residual failures on independently composed tests. Failures also persist under an unchanged contract when all proposals are rejected and the failed incumbent remains active. Starting from shared failures, editing the initial implementation instead of the failed one improves recovery, although the advantage varies across editors. Full validation and validated rollback end with fully correct programs in the core trajectory study while retaining over 43% deployment savings. Preserving agent safety requires checking what will run under current conditions and choosing which implementation to edit next."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dfba1285497dfa5be46bb8ba4907da8c0730cb0e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.00948", "CorpusId": 292521838}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dfba1285497dfa5be46bb8ba4907da8c0730cb0e", "title": "GUI-HARVEST: Self-Improving GUI Agents through Evidence-Driven Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00948, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506352681", "name": "Ge-Yi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2367194458", "name": "Zi-Kun Qu"}, {"authorId": "2383083603", "name": "Xiang Li"}, {"authorId": "2343671159", "name": "Zhi-Yong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383307252", "name": "Min Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2505389921", "name": "Shi-Pei Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2346815014", "name": "Zhong-Xiang Dai"}], "abstract": "The executable harness surrounding a GUI model determines how observations are assembled, actions are executed, and verification, recovery, and termination are controlled. Compared with harness optimization for non-GUI agents, automatically optimizing this harness poses three coupled challenges: reconciling model intent with observed visual effects, diagnosing failures under variable execution outcomes, and identifying recurrent failure patterns across tasks and translating them into reusable runtime changes. We introduce GUI-HARVEST, an automatic harness optimizer that enables self-improving GUI agents with frozen backbone models. First, to ground diagnosis in observed action effects, it aligns model outputs and executed actions with before-and-after screenshots, tying findings to specific interface transitions. Second, to account for execution variability, it treats repeated runs of the same task as a joint evidence unit, using within-task comparisons to locate outcome-relevant behavioral differences. Third, it consolidates verified findings across tasks into recurring failure patterns, maps them to bounded source-code edits with predictions recorded before evaluation, and checks the predicted behavioral effects alongside task performance through repeated execution. Experiments on OSWorld-Verified show consistent held-out gains across six general-purpose open, GUI-specialized open, and proprietary backbone models; Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct gains 12.33 points on the full suite. Frozen-harness transfer improves GPT-5 by 13.87 percentage points on WindowsAgentArena at 50 steps without further optimization. With the same backbone and initial harness, GUI-HARVEST outperforms Self-Harness and Meta-Harness, suggesting that GUI-specific diagnosis and validation help harness improvements generalize to unseen tasks. The code is available at https://github.com/GaryYang12345/GUI-HARVEST."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "37f597663234330e0ae700f74058f3c00a43da0e", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-39148", "ArXiv": "2609.39148", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.39148", "CorpusId": 292486540}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/37f597663234330e0ae700f74058f3c00a43da0e", "title": "Do Self-Evolving Skills Generalize to Held-Out Tasks?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39148, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2306254550", "name": "Xi-Hao Piao"}, {"authorId": "2108733162", "name": "Zifeng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2467891170", "name": "Zhen Chen"}], "abstract": "AI agents can externalize what they learn from past tasks into reusable \\emph{skills}, such as procedures, checklists, code, or other executable artifacts, that can be retrieved and reused when solving new tasks. Self-evolving skill methods keep rewriting these skills after each round of practice on training tasks, and the skill is then used on new tasks of the same kind. We ask a question: does the improvement a skill shows on its training tasks carry over to new test tasks? We test five self-evolving methods and a one-shot skill on six benchmarks, with the same model, the same agent, and the same train/test split for every method. Of the 21 skills that improve on their training tasks, 5 keep all of that improvement on the test tasks, 13 keep part of it, and 3 keep none of it. No existing method is best everywhere. When we read the skills, the ones that carry over badly often fix details that should depend on the task, such as column names and output files, or turn a fix for one failure into a rule for every task. An LLM judge that reads the skill content can often see this: it ranks finished skills the same way the test results do in 86\\% of pairs. But it predicts the effect of a single edit poorly, so edits still have to be tested by running them. Based on these findings, we describe Generalizable Skill Optimization (GSO), which keeps only a guide for writing skills and writes a new skill for each task; it scores highest on all six benchmarks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e6824981bbd2fcd793b245b1b6fa1d65b1278168", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-37968", "ArXiv": "2609.37968", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.37968", "CorpusId": 292474846}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e6824981bbd2fcd793b245b1b6fa1d65b1278168", "title": "SelfSearch: Reward-Free Search for Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37968, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505792679", "name": "Jungwoo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2382930292", "name": "Injin Kong"}, {"authorId": "2067643024", "name": "Yohan Jo"}], "abstract": "Advances in the coding capabilities of LLM agents allow them to inspect and modify their own instructions, tools, and execution procedures. Existing approaches use this ability to search for improved agents through repeated downstream evaluation, which incurs substantial costs and ties the search to the evaluated tasks. We introduce \\textbf{SelfSearch}, a reward-free search procedure in which agents modify themselves using records of previous self-improvement episodes. These records capture the reasoning, tool actions, and outcomes of earlier modification attempts, providing concrete experience for improving both task solving and self-modification. Without downstream reward signals during search, SelfSearch improves population-mean success over the initial agent in all six model--benchmark settings, with individual agents gaining up to 11.2 percentage points on Terminal-Bench 2.1. On SWE-bench Multilingual, an agent improves success by \\textbf{5.0} percentage points while reducing execution cost by \\textbf{38.5}\\% on tasks solved by both the initial and evolved agents. SelfSearch achieves competitive task success with evaluation-guided search baselines at lower search cost. With only \\textbf{\\$4.03} in search cost, it produces a harness that solves \\textbf{82.0}\\% of Terminal-Bench 2.1 tasks with DeepSeek V4 Flash under the settings of a public nine-harness comparison, matching the top-scoring harness, Codex. These results suggest that experience gained through self-modification can improve agents'downstream capabilities and efficiency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "049c7926dfbcec3b234609d5c998130fe8c85647", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.37950", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-37950", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.37950", "CorpusId": 292474199}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/049c7926dfbcec3b234609d5c998130fe8c85647", "title": "Video-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement of Video Understanding Agents via Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37950, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2397381548", "name": "Bin Luo"}, {"authorId": "2453690401", "name": "Jia-Lin Guo"}, {"authorId": "2500979302", "name": "Si-Qi Li"}], "abstract": "Video understanding agents acquire evidence through an executable harness that controls what they observe and how they use those observations. However, execution traces contain only the evidence acquired by the current harness, leaving competing explanations for failure unresolved and limiting the basis for self-improvement. We introduce Video-RSI, a framework for recursive self-improvement in which a video understanding agent uses its own language model to revise its harness. Through active video investigation, the model revisits the original training videos to test competing failure explanations with additional observations, grounding proposed changes in evidence beyond the existing trace. Cost-aware harness evolution turns these diagnoses into reusable revisions and determines which revisions to retain by considering both answer accuracy and visual cost. Across our evaluation settings on video understanding benchmarks, the evolved agent improves accuracy while processing fewer frames and achieves competitive accuracy-efficiency trade-offs against existing video understanding agents. These results demonstrate the potential for video understanding agents to improve their own evidence acquisition and use through harness evolution. Code is available at https://github.com/bingjunluo/Video-RSI ."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "39934f1101f3435e140237f785355b8512caed16", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-37055", "ArXiv": "2609.37055", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.37055", "CorpusId": 292474384}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/39934f1101f3435e140237f785355b8512caed16", "title": "Spatial-OPSD: Self-Improving Spatial Reasoning via Label-Free Self-Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37055, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2363415082", "name": "Zhen-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2316523566", "name": "Zhangquan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2352271005", "name": "Ke-Yi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2266170221", "name": "Ming-Ze Sun"}, {"authorId": "2054941340", "name": "Xiang An"}, {"authorId": "2171371617", "name": "Hao-Dong Jing"}, {"authorId": "2266000155", "name": "Ru-Qi Huang"}], "abstract": "Vision-language models (VLMs) increasingly operate in embodied and spatially grounded settings, where accurate understanding of depth, viewpoint, and three-dimensional relations is essential. However, improving spatial reasoning typically relies on ground-truth answers, answer-derived rewards, or other forms of task-specific supervision. We introduce Spatial-OPSD, a label-free self-improvement framework that instead exploits spatial structure naturally available from perception and reconstruction tools. During training, a privileged teacher receives automatically obtainable spatial priors, such as depth, reconstructed 3D relations, and camera geometry, while the student observes only the original visual-language input. On trajectories sampled by the student itself, the teacher provides dense token-level supervision, allowing the student to internalize spatial knowledge without ground-truth answer labels or privileged information at inference time. To extend this supervision beyond a single round, we adopt a round-wise recursive training scheme: the teacher remains frozen within each round to provide a stable learning target, and the improved student initializes both teacher and student in the next round, where privileged spatial priors re-establish an informative teacher--student asymmetry. This enables repeated self-improvement while avoiding a rapidly moving teacher during optimization. Across four VLM families, a single round of Spatial-OPSD consistently improves the five-benchmark average, while three rounds further push a strong spatially specialized model to the open-source frontier, achieving the highest average among the open models and the best results on three of five spatial reasoning benchmarks. Our code is available at https://github.com/vermouth599/Spatial-OPSD."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "be1d18fc7a9127ed9cc2b6e30cfecba059cb7d9a", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-38372", "ArXiv": "2609.38372", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.38372", "CorpusId": 292477315}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/be1d18fc7a9127ed9cc2b6e30cfecba059cb7d9a", "title": "Self-Evolving Harness on Multiple Tasks with the Agent as Its Own Optimizer", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38372, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506255064", "name": "Qian-Kai Xu"}], "abstract": "A harness is the code around a language-model agent that organizes prompts, calls tools, manages context, and controls execution. As models grow stronger, recent work has begun to let agents improve their own harnesses, a line of work known as self-evolving harnesses. In most existing methods, a separate proposer running on a human-designed harness modifies the solver's harness, and a separate harness is evolved for each benchmark. Real-world tasks come from many domains, so both the evolution and the evaluation of a harness should cover a diverse range of tasks. We propose a framework close to recursive self-improvement: the same frozen model, on the same version of the harness, first solves tasks as the solver and then, as the proposer, reads the complete run records and directly edits the harness that runs it. Each evolution batch draws tasks from five benchmarks in different domains. To measure generalization, training and held-out tasks are strictly separated, and we additionally evaluate on five out-of-distribution benchmarks never used during evolution. We frame the evolution process as deep-learning training with two stages, multi-task pretraining and continual training. Starting from a 49-line seed harness, the harness obtained at the end of the first stage improves the average score by 4.48 points on the in-distribution benchmarks and by 12.64 points on the out-of-distribution benchmarks, surpassing Codex on the former and matching it on the latter. In the second stage, continued evolution on Claw-Eval, one of the out-of-distribution benchmarks, further raises the score on that benchmark from 66.17 to 68.06, exceeding Codex. We also provide an in-depth analysis of the mechanisms that emerged during evolution, including output truncation, history compaction, and independent review."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e8cc23cd882f0bd5c16ba85742a459134ae33b73", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35738", "ArXiv": "2609.35738", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35738", "CorpusId": 292435301}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e8cc23cd882f0bd5c16ba85742a459134ae33b73", "title": "Harness Learning Enables Generalizable Test-Time Adaptation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35738, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2153660362", "name": "Alvin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "46521769", "name": "Xue-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2501709774", "name": "Zi-Xuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "83137609", "name": "Fahim Tajwar"}, {"authorId": "2138481119", "name": "Daman Arora"}, {"authorId": "2280902225", "name": "Ruslan Salakhutdinov"}, {"authorId": "2319593572", "name": "Daniel Khashabi"}, {"authorId": "2346053434", "name": "Yu-Da Song"}, {"authorId": "2288537572", "name": "Andrea Zanette"}], "abstract": "A language-model agent is jointly defined by its model and its harness, the executable program that organizes model calls, tool use, and information flow. Because different tasks call for different ways of organizing these operations, the harness needs to be adapted using feedback from the task at hand. We introduce harness learning, which trains a proposer model to revise a solver's harness using execution feedback. We formulate this process as meta-learning over executable programs, with harness revisions playing the role of weight updates in gradient-based adaptation. We train the proposer with reinforcement learning, using the task performance of revised harnesses as the reward. At test time, the proposer uses feedback from successive executions on a new task to refine the harness, without performing any parameter-space update. Experiments on reasoning and multi-hop question answering show that harness learning improves revision quality and that the ability to adapt at test time transfers to unseen tasks. Policies trained on individual revisions can continue improving harnesses over multiple rounds, while the benefits of training on revision sequences vary across settings. These findings suggest a path towards continually learning agents that turn accumulated experience into generalizable improvements."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "15cf91d563181736f5ab4b8d7381fcf0f9345e70", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.35530", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35530", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35530", "CorpusId": 292435867}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/15cf91d563181736f5ab4b8d7381fcf0f9345e70", "title": "AutoRef: Harness Optimization for Agentic Multi-Reference Image Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35530, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2290911034", "name": "Yuta Oshima"}, {"authorId": "2430636456", "name": "Ku Onoda"}, {"authorId": "1715282", "name": "Yusuke Iwasawa"}, {"authorId": "2327006455", "name": "Masahiro Suzuki"}, {"authorId": "2320464508", "name": "Yutaka Matsuo"}, {"authorId": "2052903664", "name": "Hiroki Furuta"}], "abstract": "Recent image generation models can take multiple reference images as input and combine them into a new image. However, multi-reference image generation remains challenging: models may omit or duplicate subjects from the references, or produce images in which multiple subjects appear unnaturally pasted. Recent work has proposed image generation agents that combine image generation models, reasoning models, and a harness, which is an executable program that specifies how reference images are interpreted, how generation is performed, how outputs are diagnosed, and how the final image is selected. In multi-reference generation, however, references play different roles and outputs must satisfy many criteria at once, such as fidelity to each reference and the naturalness of the whole image, so many parts of the harness could be improved, from how references are processed to how outputs are diagnosed. This makes it hard to predict which changes will improve performance and by how much, and good harnesses difficult to design by hand; indeed, human-written harnesses vary widely in performance. We therefore propose AutoRef, which optimizes the harness automatically while keeping both models frozen: a coding agent iteratively rewrites the harness code. AutoRef separates the tasks whose feedback informs proposals from the tasks used to select candidates, and continues the search from a beam of the top-ranked harnesses on the selection tasks. Using this procedure, we discover AutoRef-Harness, which improves the open-weight FLUX.2 [klein] 4B from 5.72 to 7.37 on held-out four-reference tasks of the MultiBanana benchmark, matching or exceeding proprietary models including Nano Banana Pro and GPT-Image-1.5. Without re-optimization, the same harness also improves results when the generator, number of references, benchmark, evaluator, or reasoning model differs from those used in the search."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0f33e5c389e5a33097e5c83aa6bdd81f2b24fba9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.35261", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35261", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35261", "CorpusId": 292435308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0f33e5c389e5a33097e5c83aa6bdd81f2b24fba9", "title": "Imprint Reader: From Weight-Update Readout to Behavioral Intervention", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35261, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2478723696", "name": "Guan-Xu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2374114378", "name": "Qi-Hao Lin"}, {"authorId": "2384028263", "name": "Jing Shao"}], "abstract": "As language models take a growing role in AI development, a natural aspiration is for them to reflect on their own learning process, as humans do, and use that reflection to improve themselves. At the same time, these models have an advantage that human learners lack, since training leaves parameter-level traces that can, in principle, be inspected directly. However, current models cannot decode these traces into an explicit account of what they have learned. To this end, we introduce the \\textit{Imprint Reader}, a model trained with \\textit{Semantic Mount-and-Read Tuning} (SaRT) to describe frozen weight updates. SMaRT mounts each update onto the Reader and uses an anchor-free meta-query to elicit a natural-language description, while no-change and random-perturbation controls discourage unsupported claims. On held-out updates, the joint Reader reaches judge-based Pass@100 of $2\\%$ for knowledge and $16\\%$ for behavior. These results demonstrate the feasibility of natural-language readout while pointing to reliability across updates as the next step. Beyond free-form generation, the Reader provides a differentiable proxy for the gap between a specified target behavior and a candidate weight update. Its coordinate-aligned gradients support intervention through MetaEdit. At a $0.5\\%$ pruning rate, Reader-guided selection raises measured harmful-prompt refusal from $57.9\\%$ to $64.1\\%$ under a safety-maintenance target. Using behavior descriptions without target-task training data, MetaEdit increases the frequency of backtracking and sub-goal expressions in mathematical reasoning traces and raises BFCL Overall from $41.69\\%$ to $44.60\\%$."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d80791eac1f0885ae2bebb58a29cd7fd9f37ba26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.34126", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34126", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34126", "CorpusId": 292436517}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d80791eac1f0885ae2bebb58a29cd7fd9f37ba26", "title": "JET: Judge-Guided Evolution at Test Time for Agent Programs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34126, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2464673177", "name": "Yao-Long Teng"}, {"authorId": "2505394581", "name": "Jia-Yi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2359452955", "name": "Bo An"}], "abstract": "An agent's executable program governs how it uses tools, processes observations, and responds to failures. Evolving this program at test time can help adaptation, but deciding which changes to retain is difficult when true rewards are unavailable. Execution traces provide evidence of agent behavior, yet interpreting that evidence requires a judge that remains useful as tasks and candidate programs change. We introduce Judge-Guided Evolution at Test Time (JET), which evolves an executable judge on labeled source trajectories, then freezes and transfers it to guide target-side program evolution. The judge supplies scores and diagnostic feedback without target evaluator access or model-weight updates. On unseen WebShop tasks, JET achieves approximately 13% higher mean reward than fixed-rubric guidance when evolution begins from an unevolved program (cold start) and 4% higher when it begins from one already optimized on source tasks (warm start), with a 36% relative improvement in cold-start exact success. An exact-judge control on PushT, where the judge reconstructs the scoring rule from observations, shows that without judge error, program search becomes the bottleneck. Analyses identify useful reward-prediction logic in the evolved code and show that better final selection alone cannot explain the gains. These results support executable judge transfer for program adaptation under evaluator-preserving task shifts."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "45b87e4e907d5a923a94aa05bbc3329e9d9498e9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-36393", "ArXiv": "2609.36393", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.36393", "CorpusId": 292441665}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/45b87e4e907d5a923a94aa05bbc3329e9d9498e9", "title": "Reward-rate Policy Gradient for Efficient Machine Learning Engineering Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36393, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2193553349", "name": "Mu-Hang Tian"}, {"authorId": "2360956137", "name": "Sherry Yang"}], "abstract": "Traditional reinforcement learning (RL) techniques focus on maximizing expected cumulative reward, where each action assumes to take a constant unit of time. However, this assumption does not hold for agentic RL tasks such as machine learning engineering (MLE) agents, where actions involve data loading, feature engineering, and model training that take variable durations. Efficiency matters in modern agentic RL where actions are costly. To address this limitation, we adapt from continuous-time RL and Semi-Markov Decision Process (SMDP) formulation and propose Reward-rate Policy Gradient (RPG), where we focus on optimizing the reward rate -- the long-term reward per unit of time. RPG estimates the reward rate from off-policy samples, then charges each action for the time it consumes at that rate. We first conduct theoretical analysis in the bandit setting to establish that RPG approximates the optimal reward rate and empirically demonstrate it outperforms baselines while avoiding enumeration over the policy space, a known issue for an existing method. We then further apply RPG on a small language model (Qwen3.5-4B) with self-improvement loops and empirically show it obtains higher rewards within a fixed time budget than vanilla RL on MLE-Bench and NanoGPT, with a 19.2% and 85.7% margin, respectively. Our method provides a practical solution for optimizing performance under wait time considerations in modern agentic RL tasks, where actions interact with external environments and cost time."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8e085fbdf22daa8a4518bd80d1b08511afb5fe39", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.33398", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-33398", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.33398", "CorpusId": 292436534}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8e085fbdf22daa8a4518bd80d1b08511afb5fe39", "title": "COEVO: Co-Evolving Context and Parameters for Recursive Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33398, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2144337985", "name": "Si-Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2505392749", "name": "Xin-Ping Bao"}, {"authorId": "2239169372", "name": "Xin-Yu Cai"}, {"authorId": "145144022", "name": "Yuan Cao"}, {"authorId": "2505393927", "name": "Wan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2292342129", "name": "Shao-Hong Chen"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) seeks to move large language models beyond static training pipelines toward systems that can participate in improving their own future behavior. Existing approaches largely follow two directions: updating model parameters through online learning, or improving the external context through search, reflection, and prompt optimization. Although both mechanisms can support continued improvement, they are typically studied independently. This separation overlooks an important interaction: the context shapes the experience from which a model learns, while an evolving model may interpret and utilize the same context differently over time. We therefore formulate RSI as a problem of parameter--context co-evolution, where model parameters and the learning context adapt within a shared feedback loop. We introduce COEVO, a framework that updates model parameters from on-policy experience while adapting contextual guidance according to the state of the evolving policy. Policy entropy and prompt-conditioned attention are used as complementary signals to guide this adaptation. Experiments show that COEVO consistently improves task performance over fixed-context reinforcement learning and produces policies that are more robust to changes in system prompts. More broadly, our results suggest that external context should be viewed not merely as a fixed interface to a large language model, but as an adaptive component of recursive self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eeeb6e114b77c5f297ce00f4a17c9ce37770458e", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-33180", "ArXiv": "2609.33180", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.33180", "CorpusId": 292436719}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eeeb6e114b77c5f297ce00f4a17c9ce37770458e", "title": "Which Self-Improvements Should We Trust? Reliable Self-Improvement When Agents Reuse Their Benchmarks", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33180, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Mathematics"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260618035", "name": "Xiao-Jing Sun"}, {"authorId": "2500969561", "name": "Yu-Hang Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2434938152", "name": "Zi-Hua She"}, {"authorId": "2506279353", "name": "Xiao Wang"}], "abstract": "As recursive self-improvement (RSI) rapidly advances, reliable evaluation becomes critical for guiding adaptive search. RSI typically relies on finite evaluation resources, such as fixed benchmarks, to determine which modifications are retained and what is proposed next. However, when these finite resources are repeatedly reused, new candidates are proposed based on feedback from the same evaluation set, so the search trajectory can adaptively overfit and empirical improvement may not reflect genuine population improvement on the underlying task distribution. Some existing methods account for multiple comparisons but assume that candidates are chosen independently of the evaluation set, and therefore do not control this adaptive dependence. To address this, we propose REUSE (Risk-controlled Evaluation Under Sequential Evolution), a certified evaluation and promotion framework that allows a fixed evaluation set to support repeated adaptive decisions while providing statistical guarantees. For a user-specified error level $\\alpha$, with probability at least $1-\\alpha$, every promoted modification is a genuine population improvement on the underlying task distribution. REUSE achieves this by strictly limiting the evaluation feedback returned to the search process and accounting for possible promotion histories within the error budget. We develop detailed statistical theory for RSI evaluation in this setting, including simultaneous error control, valid lower bounds on cumulative improvement, and a characterization of the fundamental limits of adaptive evaluation reuse. In live self-improvement experiments, REUSE commits substantially fewer false promotions than evaluation frameworks from current RSI systems and error-controlled baselines, reducing the proportion of false promotions from up to 20.7% to 0%, while achieving final true population performance comparable to the best baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d04712ec59b89952e6b73f7130dfbc63302e9251", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-33867", "ArXiv": "2609.33867", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.33867", "CorpusId": 292435866}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d04712ec59b89952e6b73f7130dfbc63302e9251", "title": "R2 Flow: Recursive Self-Improvement via Recursive Skill Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33867, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2358581757", "name": "Ming-Da Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2499935314", "name": "Qian-Shuo Huang"}, {"authorId": "2505394988", "name": "Yan-Jin Li"}, {"authorId": "2505800223", "name": "Zi-Jia Wang"}, {"authorId": "144562160", "name": "Qi-Ka Lin"}, {"authorId": "2376127536", "name": "Xiao-Ying Tang"}, {"authorId": "2189825927", "name": "Tiesunlong Shen"}], "abstract": "LLM-based agents can improve themselves across tasks by reusing and revising the skills they orchestrate into executable procedures. Flow-based training fits this loop: it samples procedures in proportion to reward, and the flow through each skill credits it for the next library revision. Three obstacles stand in the way of making this self-improvement reliable: flow training suffers strategy collapse over tree-structured histories; nonnegative flow-based credit rewards frequent use as if it were benefit; and library edits rest on the task reward the policy optimizes. We introduce R$^2$ Flow, a recursive self-improvement framework that alternates policy learning, independent verification, and versioned skill-library updates on a shared-state orchestration graph. The graph merges histories that differ only in the order of independent steps, allowing flow training to pool evidence across equivalent executions. A flow-share readout of the trained flow, invariant to the backward policy, and a separate signed utility rank which skills to change, verifier evidence decides whether an edit is warranted, and a residual-variance plateau sets when to update. Committed edits reshape the graph the next policy learns on, realizing recursive skill evolution. Across question answering, mathematical reasoning, interactive decision making, and code generation, R$^2$ Flow improves task accuracy and library-edit precision over heuristic orchestration, reinforcement learning, and skill-evolution baselines, and transfers across executors. Code is available at https://github.com/beita6969/r2flow."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "51a39ca712cc9008a3095d116eb82083c0eef903", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-31186", "ArXiv": "2609.31186", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.31186", "CorpusId": 292363308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/51a39ca712cc9008a3095d116eb82083c0eef903", "title": "Evolutionary Safety of Recursive Self-Improving AI: Taxonomy, Risk Discovery, and Evaluation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31186, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2057185610", "name": "Chang Gong"}, {"authorId": "2301157656", "name": "Jing-Ping Bi"}, {"authorId": "2301160943", "name": "Di Yao"}, {"authorId": "134880477", "name": "Xin-Jian Liang"}, {"authorId": "2495054480", "name": "Chao-Zhao Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2446292686", "name": "Rui Guo"}], "abstract": "Rapid advances in AI are bringing recursive self-improvement (RSI) closer to practice, with growing progress in model self-training, self-evolving agents, and automated AI development. Yet the very mechanisms that enable continuous improvement also introduce new challenges for AI safety. Unlike isolated failures, safety risks can persist across updates, accumulate through repeated adaptation, and propagate to successor systems. More importantly, the processes that generate and select future improvements may themselves evolve, making safety increasingly difficult to assess and maintain. This raises a fundamental question, i.e., how can safety be maintained when both AI systems and the processes that improve them continue to change? Therefore, we introduce Evolutionary Safety to study how safety properties are preserved, weakened, or restored throughout persistent and recursive self-improvement. We characterize six recurring manifestations of evolutionary risk and develop a five-domain taxonomy that captures how safety-relevant changes arise, persist, and propagate across evolving systems. Building on this foundation, we establish a framework for risk discovery and evaluation across states, updates, trajectories, and lineages, and examine governance principles for controlling modifications, verifying and authorizing updates, and ensuring traceability and recovery. We further identify open problems in maintaining safety as both AI systems and their improvement mechanisms continue to evolve."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d78e25873f96443da947e73c8960ce59140b6396", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-26457", "ArXiv": "2609.26457", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.26457", "CorpusId": 292227211}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d78e25873f96443da947e73c8960ce59140b6396", "title": "Recursive self-improvement of AI research agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26457, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2154616380", "name": "Dhruv Srikanth"}, {"authorId": "2305546992", "name": "Bingchen Zhao"}, {"authorId": "20492032", "name": "Di-Xing Xu"}, {"authorId": "39417610", "name": "Yuxiang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280175234", "name": "Z. Jiang"}], "abstract": "AI agents are beginning to automate research and development across the AI stack, from improving training efficiency to optimizing inference. A natural next step is to improve the research efficiency of the agents themselves. When an AI research agent's own code is the object of optimization, each accepted rewrite becomes the agent that the next round edits. We refer to this loop as recursive self-improvement. Its significance lies in a long-standing trend, in which increased cumulative spending on R&D yields diminishing returns. Sustained self-improvement offers a way to counter this trend. We present AIDE^2, a system that implements this loop for a frontier AI research agent. It proposes changes to its own code, benchmarks modified versions of itself on a suite of AI R&D tasks, and keeps the changes that perform best on hidden evaluations. In an autonomous 8-day run, AIDE^2 discovered seven successive improvements, ranging from a new search policy to memory mechanisms that compress and manage the agent's growing context. These gains generalize to four held-out benchmarks spanning machine learning engineering, heuristic algorithm engineering, and physics-based weather forecasting, the last of which is out of distribution from the selection tasks. On all four, the strongest discovered agent matches or exceeds a human-engineered production research agent that ranks among the strongest on FML-Bench. On a separate held-out task family, the discovered agents also exhibit reduced reward hacking, a property the loop never explicitly optimized for: the rate falls from 55% to 32% during the run, 7 percentage points below the human-engineered agent. Together, these results show that an AI research agent can improve its own research efficiency through recursive self-improvement, and that these gains transfer to tasks and domains the loop never encountered."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d848e629d04cdb52d359b32fb0ba1655c3a7a81", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-26891", "ArXiv": "2609.26891", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.26891", "CorpusId": 292262731}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d848e629d04cdb52d359b32fb0ba1655c3a7a81", "title": "Harness as a Language: A Minimalist Agent Framework With Maximal Expressivity", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26891, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2306053271", "name": "Zhe-Ning Li"}, {"authorId": "2478687009", "name": "Joshua Liu"}, {"authorId": "2305966049", "name": "M. Vukelic"}, {"authorId": "2186659641", "name": "Nicole Shen"}, {"authorId": "2379935238", "name": "Supriya Lall"}, {"authorId": "2070335783", "name": "A. Thakur"}, {"authorId": "2324917699", "name": "Alex L. Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2328331404", "name": "Omar Khattab"}, {"authorId": "2256983813", "name": "Jonathan Light"}, {"authorId": "2293300614", "name": "Armando Solar-Lezama"}], "abstract": "Modern language-model agents are built around the agent loop: the LLM is placed in an environment exposing a set of tools, and the LLM has full control over the workflow by alternating between tool calls and observing their output. However, certain capabilities such as long-term memory and self-improvement currently require specialized systems beyond the agent loop itself. We built an LLM agent framework, JAZ, to explore the extent to which a minimal harness that is little more than the agent loop itself can accomplish tasks these specialized systems are built for. JAZ exposes a single LLM-based primitive `invoke` and provides a set of built-in hooks that allow the programmer to apply constraints and perform monitoring. Generalizing existing code-mode agent loops, `invoke` is the simplest loop that satisfies two defining properties: (1) the LLM can write arbitrary executable code that can include recursive `invoke`; (2) everything visible to the LLM - all inputs to `invoke` as well as its interaction history with the code environment - are variables in the code environment. We motivate our design from first principles, viewing `invoke` as a language primitive representing a function whose implementation is provided at runtime by an LLM every time it is called. To validate the design of our core `invoke` primitive, we evaluate `invoke` - with only prompting, no manually designed tools, harness, or external systems (e.g., memory or the file system) - on workflows traditionally implemented through specialized harnesses. On long-horizon workflows requiring recall far beyond the context window, JAZ `invoke` outperforms Letta (MemGPT) by 8% at half its cost on the recall-heavy portion of StuLife. On continual self-improvement, JAZ `invoke` outperforms ACE by 4% at a lower cost on AppWorld."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "68354d1f432906ab68fec4579f8b32f7fa07efbd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.26863", "CorpusId": 292263003}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/68354d1f432906ab68fec4579f8b32f7fa07efbd", "title": "Towards Strategy-Level RSI for Skill-Augmented Agents: Learning When to Reuse Skills from Execution Feedback", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26863, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2495960759", "name": "Shuang-Quan Guo"}, {"authorId": "2509162188", "name": "Xiao-Bo Sui"}, {"authorId": "2509294034", "name": "Fei Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2260298899", "name": "Rui-Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2494113774", "name": "Man-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2504133561", "name": "Rong-Xue Li"}, {"authorId": "2509162123", "name": "Zi-Yang Ou"}], "abstract": "Long-running agents accumulate reusable Skills, but a Skill that is semantically relevant to a task is not necessarily worth loading in the current state. We study the applicability question that arises once a candidate Skill is known: should it be loaded in the current state? We propose SkillApt, which uses matched WITH/WITHOUT Skill executions on the same task state as persistent evidence, estimates the conditional marginal utility of the Skill, and chooses LOAD or ABSTAIN accordingly. The base model, agent architecture, and Skill contents stay fixed; only the external deployment policy changes. We call this constrained setting strategy-level recursive self-improvement (Strategy-Level RSI). On 20 Skills and 160 held-out states, as paired evidence accumulates, SkillApt's task success rises from 81.9% under a cold start to 91.3%, matching a strong zero-shot LLM controller; yet SkillApt activates Skills on only 26.3% of states, versus 98.8% for the zero-shot controller. A hard-candidate study shows that non-optimal Skills mostly leave correctness unchanged while raising execution cost, and occasionally cause correctness harm. An ablation shows that a history recording only WITH success makes the policy load almost everywhere, whereas paired evidence substantially improves selectivity. These results indicate that relevance is not applicability: the main effect of execution evidence is not to make the model stronger but to change how existing Skills are deployed, moving the system from near-always loading to selective reuse."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "416892784199fdc62ad778b888a66335693aa0ef", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-24130", "ArXiv": "2609.24130", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.24130", "CorpusId": 292218732}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/416892784199fdc62ad778b888a66335693aa0ef", "title": "Self-Healing Harness for Runtime Oversight of Agent Self-Modification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24130, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2243275419", "name": "Sina Tayebati"}, {"authorId": "2282102714", "name": "Divake Kumar"}, {"authorId": "2140463925", "name": "Nastaran Darabi"}, {"authorId": "2343838580", "name": "Ranganath Krishnan"}, {"authorId": "2242437952", "name": "A. Trivedi"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can change their own future behavior, raising a basic control question of which self-generated changes should be allowed to persist. We formulate this as admission control for self-modification. The agent may propose changes to its operating instructions, while an external runtime gate controls persistence. We implement this principle as a model-agnostic self-healing harness that runs a Detect, Notice, Heal, Validate loop around an otherwise unmodified agent. The agent authors candidate behavioral rules in an external workspace, where they receive provisional execution authority during evaluation and acquire persistent cross-episode authority only after measured improvement on the triggering failure without regression beyond a fixed margin on protected cases. Replay provides matched evidence when available, forward trials provide a weaker fallback, and a corpus-level guard re-tests the accumulated active rule set. Across 16 matched Baseline and Harness runs spanning AppWorld, Terminal-Bench, and $\\tau^2$-Bench, the gate rejected 383 replay-decided proposals. Of these, 211 (55%) improved their triggering failure while degrading a case that previously worked. This shows that locally beneficial self-modifications can introduce collateral regressions often enough to materially affect gate decisions, providing direct empirical motivation for external admission control. Task-completion score is higher under the Harness in all 16 pairs, with two paired bootstrap intervals excluding zero, while repeated-trial reliability is higher in 12 pairs, tied in 4, and lower in none. Because adaptation modifies the policy-inducing context while leaving model weights fixed, admitted changes remain inspectable, reversible, and compatible with closed-weight models."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cb9ae0fa63b05c0ddfda0b078c7001640467bea6", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-24838", "ArXiv": "2609.24838", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.24838", "CorpusId": 292220442}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cb9ae0fa63b05c0ddfda0b078c7001640467bea6", "title": "MedRSI: Recursive Self-Improvement for Medical Agents via Clinically Aligned Self-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24838, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362231239", "name": "Jun-De Wu"}, {"authorId": "2275784890", "name": "Jia-Yuan Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2410293305", "name": "Min-Hao Hu"}, {"authorId": "2482301907", "name": "Feng-Lin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2048020438", "name": "Jia-Zhen Pan"}], "abstract": "Medical agents increasingly combine general reasoning models with specialized clinical tools, yet their capabilities remain largely fixed by what clinicians and engineers design before deployment. Recursive self-improvement (RSI) offers a different paradigm in which agents learn from their own failures and autonomously expand their capabilities, but directly applying RSI to medicine introduces fundamental safety challenges. We introduce MedRSI, the first recursive self-improvement framework for medicine, which continuously transforms diagnostic failures into new clinical capabilities through tool composition and task-specific model training. Inspired by clinical practice, MedRSI introduces two mechanisms for clinically aligned self-evolution. Clinical-cost-aware failure prioritization directs improvement toward errors according to their potential clinical consequences rather than frequency alone. Fast discovery with slow registration separates rapid capability invention from conservative adoption, allowing new tools to enter the persistent agent only after demonstrating sustained benefit across subsequent patient cohorts. Across public glaucoma and heart disease benchmarks and two private clinical tasks, MedRSI progressively develops segmentation, measurement, prediction, multimodal reasoning, and generative capabilities, surpasses manually engineered medical agents, and autonomously discovers solutions to clinical problems not anticipated by its original designers. Our results show that medical agents need not remain constrained by capabilities specified before deployment: with clinically grounded mechanisms governing what to improve and what to retain, they can continuously construct, validate, and accumulate new capabilities from diagnostic experience. Code is available at https://github.com/ImprintLab/MedRSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "70b5ade593b5a02a324f9cc4b469c63bca7f100b", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-19664", "ArXiv": "2609.19664", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.19664", "CorpusId": 292059297}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70b5ade593b5a02a324f9cc4b469c63bca7f100b", "title": "VideoResearcher: Self-Improving Tool Design for Long-Video Understanding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19664, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2459501175", "name": "Ding-Qiang Ye"}, {"authorId": "2435059322", "name": "Dong-Di Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2307928587", "name": "Kai-Shen Wang"}, {"authorId": "2491117701", "name": "Qing-Qiao Hu"}, {"authorId": "46968861", "name": "Jing-Chen Sun"}, {"authorId": "2302821558", "name": "Yi-Jun Liang"}, {"authorId": "2260844526", "name": "Yu-Qi Jia"}, {"authorId": "2402771343", "name": "Yi-Qiao Huang"}, {"authorId": "2371374663", "name": "Yun-Jie Tian"}, {"authorId": "2499937548", "name": "Jia-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2368706330", "name": "Chuan-Yang Jin"}, {"authorId": "2307452752", "name": "Ke Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2308080382", "name": "Vishal M. Patel"}, {"authorId": "2181319179", "name": "Di Fu"}], "abstract": "Video agents have made substantial progress in long-video understanding. Yet effective video-agent systems require costly, time-consuming manual design and trial and error. Current self-improvement methods either refine low-impact prompts, recombine predefined micro-tools, or struggle with convergence in harness optimization. To bridge this gap, we target high-impact video-tool with VideoResearcher, a training-free multi-agent framework that autonomously designs, tests, and refines tools for video understanding, like a human researcher. VideoResearcher operates through dual Solving and Evolving loops: it analyzes tool-use trajectories to identify capability gaps, coordinates specialized agents to develop and validate executable tools, and reuses evolved tools to strengthen evidence acquisition in subsequent video reasoning. Through iterative tool refinement and validation, it progressively strengthens evidence acquisition without updating model parameters. VideoResearcher achieves state-of-the-art performance among self-improving agents and approaches the human-designed upper bound, demonstrating a training-free paradigm for long-video understanding that expands agent capabilities through autonomous tool development while reducing costly manual engineering."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b91ba133d7da1d6c5292c34fb3ceddce507581cf", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-19526", "ArXiv": "2609.19526", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.19526", "CorpusId": 292059081}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b91ba133d7da1d6c5292c34fb3ceddce507581cf", "title": "Self Improvement via Fast Tree-search", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19526, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2500256782", "name": "Xing Fu"}, {"authorId": "1398680355", "name": "Aravinth Kulanthaivelu"}, {"authorId": "2111746399", "name": "Yutaro Yamada"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can recursively modify their own implementations, forming a loop of self-improvement. While prior work shows this can boost performance on coding benchmarks, existing approaches are costly and compute-intensive. We introduce a simple, sample-efficient self-improvement framework that significantly improves coding performance under strict budget constraints. We identify evaluation of candidate self-modifications as the main runtime bottleneck since prior approaches estimate their effectiveness by re-running a subset of benchmark tasks with the modified agent, which is time-consuming. We introduce Recursive Self Improvement via Fast Tree-search (SIFT), which augments these downstream task evaluations with an LLM-as-a-judge signal that performs pairwise comparisons between candidate patches, where the win-loss record is aggregated with a regularized Bradley-Terry model, and the resulting strength scores drive rank-based parent sampling inside a lightweight disaggregated tree search. Expensive downstream task evaluations are reserved only for the most promising nodes. Using a fully disaggregated tree search pipeline, the judge scores provide intermediate signal to guide exploration on promising candidate patches without being bottlenecked by slow evaluation runs. SIFT outperforms existing tree-search based self-evolution frameworks on the full Polyglot benchmark with significantly lower resource requirements in terms of CPU hours, wall clock time, and API cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "434235c8ee41005969245bf0b99525eda65dd7b7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.15364", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-15364", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.15364", "CorpusId": 292027115}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/434235c8ee41005969245bf0b99525eda65dd7b7", "title": "RSIAgent: Autonomous Exploration for Recursive Self-improvement in New Environments", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15364, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2110018955", "name": "Si-Bo Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2499630629", "name": "Shi-Cheng Fan"}, {"authorId": "2313740191", "name": "Xin-Yue Wang"}, {"authorId": "2356519744", "name": "Wen-Yi Wu"}, {"authorId": "2400501576", "name": "Kun Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2363876418", "name": "Bi-Wei Huang"}], "abstract": "Digital agents must often adapt to new environments whose interfaces, tools, and failure modes are not fully captured by pretrained models. We introduce \\textbf{RSIAgent}, a training-free multi-agent framework for recursive self-improvement through autonomous memory construction. RSIAgent coordinates curriculum, actor, and verifier agents to continually explore the environment, validate outcomes, and retain environment-specific knowledge, including reusable causal relationships between actions, conditions, and consequences. It further adopts a \\textbf{broad-then-deep} exploration strategy, combining parallel broad recursive self-exploration for discovering diverse environment structures with focused deep self-exploration for uncovering hard cases, hidden constraints, boundary conditions, and previously unknown causal dependencies. The resulting memory is frozen and can be directly reused for downstream tasks without updating model parameters. Experiments on OSWorld-v2 and Agent's Last Exam show that RSIAgent substantially improves strong open-source models, enabling Kimi-K3 and GLM-5.3 to outperform frontier closed-source models including GPT-6."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f74d085f2bbba1b043b05d3b11bf34d451a2468c", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-14260", "ArXiv": "2609.14260", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.14260", "CorpusId": 292027229}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f74d085f2bbba1b043b05d3b11bf34d451a2468c", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement LLM Agents for Inverter Dynamic Model Identification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14260, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2304528606", "name": "Jie Feng"}, {"authorId": "2331495909", "name": "Xiao-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2331465334", "name": "Xin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2308038006", "name": "Yuan-Yuan Shi"}], "abstract": "This is a position paper. We demonstrate that recursive self-improvement (RSI) large language model (LLM) agents are a natural search engine for dynamic model identification of inverter-based resources (IBRs) whose internal controls are often proprietary and hidden from grid operators. White-box models provide physical transparency but require vendor disclosure; black-box models avoid this requirement but sacrifice interpretability; and existing grey-box approaches, including sparse and symbolic regression, are poorly suited to discovering feedback control architectures or incorporating control-engineering priors. Our position is that this gap can be alleviated by (1) restricting the search space to a typed vocabulary of standard control modules, including PI controllers, phase-locked loops (PLLs), low-pass filters, etc., composed under block-diagram grammar rules, and (2) using an RSI LLM agent to perform program search over candidate block-diagram models, guided by measured frequency-domain admittance data at the point of common coupling (PCC), while fitting the free parameters of each candidate by nonlinear least squares. We instantiate this position by adapting ThetaEvolve, an open-source program-evolution framework supporting in-context evolution and test-time learning, to inverter model discovery. In a proof-of-concept study on a grid-following (GFL) inverter benchmark, the RSI loop reduces the normalized root mean square error (NRMSE) of a naive open-loop model from 0.470 to 0.0435 and identifies a 15-module closed-loop structure that closely resembles the hidden ground-truth GFL controller."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a7813cba378ec5eeb2b501fce867f59bcb0c4996", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.13406", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-13406", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.13406", "CorpusId": 292012667}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a7813cba378ec5eeb2b501fce867f59bcb0c4996", "title": "Generalized Agent Iteration: One Formal Framework for Iterative Policy Improvement and Recursive Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13406, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "31190626", "name": "Hongyao Tang"}, {"authorId": "2146275908", "name": "Yi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2209966044", "name": "Peng-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2112499487", "name": "Yi-Fu Yuan"}], "abstract": "When we speak of recursive self-improvement (RSI), are we speaking of a phenomenon, a mechanism, or a prospect? Towards autonomous and evolving intelligence, RSI is being claimed at many scales, while no single framework that formally describes these emerging instances exists. Its counterpart in the classical realm, iterative policy improvement, is characterized by generalized policy iteration (GPI), a framework of broad applicability with well-understood theoretical properties, but only where the update principle and the evaluation base lie outside the agent. In this paper, we propose Generalized Agent Iteration (GAI), a formal framework that describes iterative policy improvement and RSI as two cases of a single learning paradigm. GAI defines the agent as a configuration of modifiable components within a system and models the learning process as a cycle of agent evaluation and agent improvement. Two pivotal dials then distinguish the instances, in particular the systems that self-identify as RSI in recent work: whether the improving mechanism is part of the agent and whether the standard it is measured against is grounded outside it. The former dial delineates the boundary between GPI and RSI, and the latter determines a system's polarity as anchored, goal drift, or fully self-referential. Moreover, we use these coordinates to place existing systems on the same two axes and make the defects of recursive self-improvement statable one condition at a time. We see this paper as a first step toward exploring a formal characterization of RSI that rests on the classical account, makes existing systems comparable, and provides a principled basis for analyzing and designing new ones."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e7313d7961c781e0fbf8bcb727e4d08008c7737e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.12216", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-12216", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.12216", "CorpusId": 291956099}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e7313d7961c781e0fbf8bcb727e4d08008c7737e", "title": "Guardrailed Meta-Agent Loops: Stress-Testing Policy Pinning, Budget Bounds, and Crash Recovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2494586159", "name": "Qin-Zhen Ma"}, {"authorId": "2467844427", "name": "Jia-Lin Wu"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agent workflows create an audit problem when the same controller can change both its behavior and the conditions under which that behavior is judged. We present GuardrailLoop, a simulation-based testbed that makes three operational contracts jointly testable: preservation of human-defined policy, compute accounting at every recorded execution prefix, and recovery of a specified scientific state after crashes. A hash-pinned policy fixes goals, scope, evaluation identity, budget, and release conditions; machine-directed evolution is restricted to a code-owned feature catalog and bounded knobs. The contribution is an executable boundary and an evaluation protocol that separates useful adaptation, state recovery, and repeated execution. In a paired 50-seed 2 x 2 study, round-stage growth changes target attainment by +1.00 and restricted mean compute to target by -56.97 simulated GPU-hours (95% paired-bootstrap interval [-58.91,-54.70]); idle growth has zero measured utility effect. Across 240 enumerated crash injections, all runs recover the defined outcome, but only 210 preserve the normalized trace: 30 pre-commit crashes repeat a planner call. Resource-drift, kill-switch, integrity, and output-guard matrices satisfy their specified checks. These findings show why successful outcome recovery is insufficient evidence of exactly-once execution. They establish conformance within one calibrated deterministic testbed, rather than general safety or real-world self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "40a338b117f1a0142f31c2e6b4d059f0abd41e40", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.06396", "CorpusId": 291869941}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/40a338b117f1a0142f31c2e6b4d059f0abd41e40", "title": "MetaRSI / RSI2: A Meta-Recursive Self-Improving System for Recursive Self-Improving Systems Themselves", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.06396, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497140705", "name": "Zi-Hang Tan"}, {"authorId": "2491830815", "name": "Lei-Xin Sun"}, {"authorId": "2349549831", "name": "Zi-Tong Shi"}, {"authorId": "2492549524", "name": "Yi-Tao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2491513933", "name": "Jia-Jun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2396770822", "name": "Nathaniel Brooks"}, {"authorId": "2375328320", "name": "Jia-Ru Qian"}, {"authorId": "2398907588", "name": "Xiao-Ran Shang"}, {"authorId": "2495565115", "name": "Su-Yuan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2495436771", "name": "Yi Ding"}, {"authorId": "2396663388", "name": "Yang-Xu Liao"}, {"authorId": "2456517497", "name": "Mu Li"}, {"authorId": "2324132621", "name": "Qiu-Shi Sun"}, {"authorId": "2491396033", "name": "Shu-Dong Liu"}, {"authorId": "2346838104", "name": "Xuan-Kun Rong"}, {"authorId": "2282307764", "name": "Xiao-Hang Yu"}, {"authorId": "2494257140", "name": "Zhuo Chen"}, {"authorId": "2382921746", "name": "He-Jia Geng"}, {"authorId": "2145555404", "name": "Chen-Xing Li"}, {"authorId": "2492133551", "name": "Ao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2359745491", "name": "Zeng-Ji Tu"}, {"authorId": "2493873858", "name": "Robert Tang"}, {"authorId": "2495738938", "name": "Yu-Xin Zhan"}, {"authorId": "2382935045", "name": "E. Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2491623398", "name": "Yu-Xin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2452143015", "name": "Jian-Qin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367462247", "name": "Xiao Liang"}, {"authorId": "2454976367", "name": "Fang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2473480106", "name": "Hao-Chi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2490983005", "name": "A. Marlow"}, {"authorId": "2229286959", "name": "Guan-Cheng Wan"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) lets a system improve the model-building machinery from its own failures, so every later model inherits the gain. Yet RSI has been validated almost exclusively on coding and formal benchmarks such as science QA and mathematics. This format bound limits RSI to improvement within a machine-checkable slice, not general capability where questions are open and correctness is settled by argument, replication, or measurement. We argue RSI must next operate across real, diverse scientific, engineering, and meta-scientific domains, not where formal evaluation is merely tractable. To that end we present MetaRSI-v1, where improvement is the scheduled composition of three typed operators over one unified paradigm. Data-RSI amplifies existing competence and marks its boundary; Harness-RSI edits a five-slot scaffold without touching weights; Model-RSI internalizes capability into parameters through bounded training. Sharing one loop kernel and artifact vocabulary, they make data, scaffold, and model changes composable rather than exclusive. A two-axis optimizer jointly decides operator order and each operator's proposal policy, while a meta-level policy revises the schedule across terms. We validate MetaRSI-v1 under the field's standard evaluations, on code and closed-form science, with no external teacher: the target model plays every role in its own loop. MetaRSI-v1 reframes self-improvement from a single-surface edit to a composition across the full model-production pipeline, opening two paths: a model route internalizing capability through training, and a harness route leaving weights untouched and thus extending self-improvement to any model reachable through an interface, with Data-RSI redefined as the shared substrate feeding both. The framework further yields refutable laws on where loops exist, how operators compose, and what supervision buys."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3cfc67a71e63ed7e96f412d1fe7643bdf4e26cd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.03546", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-03546", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.03546", "CorpusId": 291678205}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3cfc67a71e63ed7e96f412d1fe7643bdf4e26cd", "title": "Dalek: A Constructive Agent Machine", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03546, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2488098846", "name": "Wan-Peng Xie"}], "abstract": "We present Dalek, a closed machine designed for agents that realizes self-maintenance, self-evolution, self-reproduction, and self-organization on any substrate satisfying a general host contract. The machine is built from three primitives---actors, messages, and channels. Four obligations---a host boundary, a construction language, admissible transitions, and rule heredity---give its boundary, identity, and closure a structural basis. Von Neumann's 1948 self-reproducing automaton supplies a hereditary constructional core: a self-description together with a constructor, a copier, and a controller. Dalek combines this core with the four obligations and rederives its medium for a text-and-message agent substrate, adding explicit structures for boundary, identity, history, and growth. A large language model and a compiler occupy the payload position and form a general capability producer. New capabilities are authored, compiled, installed into the description, and inherited by descendants. The same path produces the machine's own organs and even its runtime, closing heredity and evolution within the machine."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1aed84018edfa64766b11d29bfa87a6e2c3b5db2", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-13236", "ArXiv": "2609.13236", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.13236", "CorpusId": 292012431}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1aed84018edfa64766b11d29bfa87a6e2c3b5db2", "title": "Self-Evolving AI for Humanoids: Mechanisms, Safety, and Evaluation of Post-Deployment Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13236, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2209209608", "name": "Loc X. Nguyen"}, {"authorId": "2209218438", "name": "Avi Deb Raha"}, {"authorId": "2064731560", "name": "Huy Q. Le"}, {"authorId": "2288073734", "name": "Eui-nam Huh"}, {"authorId": "2340230621", "name": "D. Niyato"}, {"authorId": "2268297857", "name": "Choong Seon Hong"}], "abstract": "Humanoid robots are becoming an important part of embodied artificial intelligence, driven by advances in reinforcement learning for locomotion, world models for prediction, and vision-language-action models for general control. However, most of these systems remain static after deployment. A policy is trained offline for a fixed objective and then frozen, even though the tasks, environments, and robot bodies keep drifting over time. An emerging paradigm of self-evolving agents aims to address this problem by allowing systems to improve from their own post-deployment experience. Since most existing studies focus on disembodied software agents, this survey examines how self-evolution changes when an agent has a physical body. We first define self-evolution for humanoids and represent a deployed robot using a state tuple that includes its policy, perception, memory, workflow, and body. This state is updated by an evolution operator in a slow outer loop with a lifelong objective. We then organize the literature into four complementary mechanisms of self-evolution, presented in increasing order of autonomy: self-learning, self-adaptation, self-optimization, and self-generation. Since changes to a humanoid can introduce physical hazards, we treat safety and uncertainty as key design dimensions of the evolution operator, and further formulate admissible evolution as a constraint enforced by a world-model verification gate within a human-oversight envelope. Finally, we present that evaluation should track the robot's evolving trajectory rather than a fixed checkpoint, and we identify the lack of a benchmark designed specifically for self-evolving humanoids. Moreover, we outline open challenges spanning AI algorithms, on-board systems, and governance."}}]}
